(:title "Graphden — developer tour"
 :mins 751.4
 :blocks
 ((:id "executor" :title "Executor — how a fn runs" :status "toured" :mins 85.7 :summary "The spine: turn a graph 'description of a computation' into a value."
   :steps
   ((:key "executor/execute" :n 1 :gi 0 :defn "execute" :ns "graphden.executor.interface" :file "src/graphden/executor/interface.clj" :line 85 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn execute" :see (("executor/create-context" . "create-context")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "**Start here.** `execute` is the public front door of the whole system. A caller hands it an execution *context*, a `fn-id` — the id of a row in the graph that *describes* a computation — and a map of free args. This ns is a thin, validating façade: it checks the args shape and delegates to the real machinery in `compile-runtime`. Everything below is how that graph *description* becomes a value.")
    (:key "executor/execute~2" :n 2 :gi 1 :defn "execute" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-runtime" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_runtime.clj" :line 1478 :lang "clj" :mins 4.0 :head "(defn execute" :see (("executor/registry" . "registry")) :refs nil :siblings (("executor/registry" . "registry") ("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!") ("platform/cloud-forbidden-effects" . "cloud-forbidden-effects"))
     :say "The dispatch heart of the hot path. It checks the cancellation / effect / execute-guard gates, pulls the **compiled registry** out of the context, looks up the closure for this `fn-id` (or throws fn-not-found), translates the caller's name-keyed args into the dual slot-id+name shape the compiled code expects, and finally calls `(closure translated ctx)`. This is the moment *execute a fn* becomes *call a precompiled closure*.")
    (:key "executor/create-context" :n 3 :gi 2 :defn "create-context" :ns "graphden.executor.context" :file "src/graphden/executor/context.clj" :line 406 :lang "clj" :mins 9.6 :head "(defn create-context" :see nil :refs (("executor/execute" . "execute") ("versioning/build-branch-ctx" . "build-branch-ctx") ("boot/context" . "context")) :siblings (("executor/fill-graph-cache!" . "fill-graph-cache!"))
     :say "Builds the `ExecutionContext` the previous step reads from. It carries the storage handle, the `:base-fns` impl registry (snapshotted from the global default), a lazily-filled `:compiled-registry` holder, the graph/deps caches, and the authz/effect seams. Because scoping (e.g. which branch) lives here, the *same* `fn-id` run in two contexts can resolve to different code — this is where that happens.")
    (:key "executor/fill-graph-cache!" :n 4 :gi 3 :defn "fill-graph-cache!" :ns "graphden.executor.context" :file "src/graphden/executor/context.clj" :line 359 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn fill-graph-cache!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("executor/create-context" . "create-context"))
     :say "How the graph cache is FILLED on a miss — and why the install is guarded. A write that commits while a loader is reading storage finds an empty cache (nothing to splice) and primes the post-write graph through its delta path; an unguarded loader would then put its pre-write read back over it, and readers would miss the fn they just created until the next write. So a loader takes the ctx's invalidation epoch BEFORE reading and installs under the invalidation lock only while that epoch still holds — a stale read is discarded, never published.")
    (:key "executor/registry" :n 5 :gi 4 :defn "registry" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-runtime" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_runtime.clj" :line 926 :lang "clj" :mins 3.0 :head "(defn registry" :see (("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!")) :refs (("executor/execute~2" . "execute") ("boot/compiled-registry" . "compiled-registry")) :siblings (("executor/execute~2" . "execute") ("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!") ("platform/cloud-forbidden-effects" . "cloud-forbidden-effects"))
     :say "Returns the `{fn-id -> closure}` map from the context holder. A warm holder is served straight back; if a full clear flagged it stale it is still served immediately while a background `rebuild-optimistic!` refreshes it (stale-while-revalidate — no reader ever blocks behind a ~50s cold compile). Only a genuinely COLD holder (boot, a divergent cold branch) compiles on demand under the invalidation lock, double- checked so concurrent callers coalesce onto one rebuild. This is the lazy **compile-once, reuse-forever** registry the executor runs against.")
    (:key "executor/rebuild!" :n 6 :gi 5 :defn "rebuild!" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-runtime" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_runtime.clj" :line 607 :lang "clj" :mins 3.5 :head "(defn rebuild!" :see (("executor/build-lookups" . "build-lookups") ("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all")) :refs (("executor/registry" . "registry") ("storage/ExecutionGraph" . "ExecutionGraph") ("boot/compiled-registry" . "compiled-registry")) :siblings (("executor/execute~2" . "execute") ("executor/registry" . "registry") ("platform/cloud-forbidden-effects" . "cloud-forbidden-effects"))
     :say "The compile-at-startup pipeline in one form: `read-graph` from storage, then `build-lookups`, then `compile-all`; finally it primes the graph/deps caches and resets the registry holder. This is the seam where **code = graph in DB** turns into runnable Clojure closures. The next few steps open up each stage it calls.")
    (:key "executor/sync-fns-to-storage!" :n 7 :gi 6 :defn "sync-fns-to-storage!" :ns "graphden.executor.composition.core" :file "src/graphden/executor/composition/core.clj" :line 339 :lang "clj" :mins 2.6 :head "(defn sync-fns-to-storage!" :see nil :refs (("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!") ("web/sync-fn-defs-branch!" . "sync-fn-defs-branch!")) :siblings nil
     :say "The opposite direction — and the reason there is a graph to read at all. Authored fn-defs (from packages) are reconciled into fn / slot / binding rows in storage here. So the graph that `rebuild!` reads was itself *written* by this composition layer from source fn-defs. Read it to see how declarations become persisted graph entities.")
    (:key "executor/build-lookups" :n 8 :gi 7 :defn "build-lookups" :ns "graphden.executor.compile.lookups" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile/lookups.clj" :line 70 :lang "clj" :mins 6.2 :head "(defn build-lookups" :see nil :refs (("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!") ("packages/resolve-slot-owner" . "resolve-slot-owner") ("editor/buildLookups" . "buildLookups")) :siblings (("executor/root-fn" . "root-fn"))
     :say "Indexes the raw graph rows — fns, slots, fn-slots, bindings, list-items — into the O(1) maps every compile helper consumes (`:fn-map`, `:slot-map`, `:bindings-by-fn`, the inheritance-chain cache, …). This is the in-memory materialization of the DB graph that the whole compile pass walks over.")
    (:key "executor/root-fn" :n 9 :gi 8 :defn "root-fn" :ns "graphden.executor.compile.lookups" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile/lookups.clj" :line 246 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defn root-fn" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("executor/build-lookups" . "build-lookups"))
     :say "Walks a composed fn's `parent-ids` inheritance chain up to the parentless **root** that owns the slots and carries a registered impl. This is the base-fn resolution step: how an arbitrary composed `fn-id` maps to the primitive whose Clojure impl will actually run.")
    (:key "executor/collect-bindings" :n 10 :gi 9 :defn "collect-bindings" :ns "graphden.executor.compile.bindings" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile/bindings.clj" :line 337 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn collect-bindings" :see (("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Classifies each of a fn's root slots (via `classify-slot`) into a `:value` / `:free` / `:ref` / `:seq` / `:secret-value` entry, memoised on the lookups. This classification is the pivot the code generator switches on next — the *shape* of every argument is decided here, once, at compile time rather than per execution.")
    (:key "executor/compile-all" :n 11 :gi 10 :defn "compile-all" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-eager" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_eager.clj" :line 1545 :lang "clj" :mins 4.8 :head "(defn compile-all" :see (("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn")) :refs (("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!") ("types/record-rich-types!" . "record-rich-types!")) :siblings (("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn") ("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder") ("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl") ("executor/call-with-cache" . "call-with-cache"))
     :say "Topologically orders every impl-backed fn and compiles each in dependency order into `{fn-id -> (fn [fa ctx])}`, cached process-wide by (graph-shape × base-fn set × ambient rich-types) — the rich-types component is load-bearing: `produces-callable?` HOF-wrap decisions depend on the swept `:return-type` entries, so callers under different types must not share a compile. Dependency order matters: by the time a parent compiles, its children's callables already exist to be wired in.")
    (:key "executor/compile-fn" :n 12 :gi 11 :defn "compile-fn" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-eager" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_eager.clj" :line 1244 :lang "clj" :mins 8.3 :head "(defn compile-fn" :see (("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder") ("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl")) :refs (("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all")) :siblings (("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all") ("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder") ("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl") ("executor/call-with-cache" . "call-with-cache"))
     :say "Produces one fn's runtime closure `(fn [fa ctx])`. It turns each classified binding into a *builder*, wires env-bindings and rename-aliases, and at call time assembles the args map and invokes `(impl args ctx)`. This is the concrete anatomy of a single compiled graph node.")
    (:key "executor/arg-builder" :n 13 :gi 12 :defn "arg-builder" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-eager" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_eager.clj" :line 1031 :lang "clj" :mins 8.8 :head "(defn- arg-builder" :see (("executor/resolve-arg" . "resolve-arg")) :refs (("executor/collect-bindings" . "collect-bindings") ("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn") ("executor/resolve-arg" . "resolve-arg") ("executor/map-fn" . "map-fn")) :siblings (("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all") ("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn") ("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl") ("executor/call-with-cache" . "call-with-cache"))
     :say "Emits the per-binding `(fn [fa ctx])`: `:value` becomes a constant, `:free` reads the slot-id (with a name fallback), `:ref` becomes a `thunk`/delay that **defers** the child call, `:seq` becomes a lazy seq. This is the origin of graphden's laziness — refs are *wrapped, not evaluated*, so native Clojure `if`/`and`/`or` short-circuit for free without the executor knowing their names.")
    (:key "executor/resolve-impl" :n 14 :gi 13 :defn "resolve-impl" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-eager" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_eager.clj" :line 791 :lang "clj" :mins 1.1 :head "(defn- resolve-impl" :see (("executor/defbase" . "defbase")) :refs (("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def")) :siblings (("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all") ("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn") ("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder") ("executor/call-with-cache" . "call-with-cache"))
     :say "Looks the base-fn's Clojure impl out of `lookups[:base-fns]` by the root fn's name, throwing `:compile/missing-impl` on a gap. This is the concrete join between a graph node and the actual code that computes it — the point where the graph description meets a real function.")
    (:key "executor/defbase" :n 15 :gi 14 :defn "defbase" :ns "graphden.executor.defbase" :file "src/graphden/executor/defbase.clj" :line 142 :lang "clj" :mins 2.6 :head "(defmacro defbase" :see (("executor/resolve-arg" . "resolve-arg")) :refs (("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl") ("packages/add" . "add")) :siblings nil
     :say "The macro base-fn authors use. It rewrites a body that names its args as bare symbols into `(defn name [__args ctx] …)`, where each symbol reads through `resolve-arg`. This explains why the impls that `resolve-impl` finds look like ordinary Clojure even though their arguments arrive as a compiled map.")
    (:key "executor/resolve-arg" :n 16 :gi 15 :defn "resolve-arg" :ns "graphden.executor.runtime" :file "src/graphden/executor/runtime.clj" :line 59 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn resolve-arg" :see (("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder")) :refs (("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder") ("executor/defbase" . "defbase")) :siblings nil
     :say "The read-side of laziness. Inside an impl this forces a `thunk` (calling it once), derefs an `IDeref`/`delay`, or returns a plain value untouched. This is where a deferred `:ref` argument actually gets evaluated — at the moment, and only if, the impl reads it. Pair it with `arg-builder`, which created the thunk.")
    (:key "executor/call-with-cache" :n 17 :gi 16 :defn "call-with-cache" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-eager" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_eager.clj" :line 704 :lang "clj" :mins 3.2 :head "(defn- call-with-cache" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all") ("executor/compile-fn" . "compile-fn") ("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder") ("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl"))
     :say "Closes the loop. Child-callable invocations are memoised for the duration of one top-level `execute`, keyed by `[ref-id projected-free-args]` in a per-call map (with a `::nil` sentinel and a size cap). This is how the lazy graph avoids recomputing the same sub-result and delivers a single cached value back up to the caller — the DRY principle, enforced at runtime.")
    (:key "executor/map-fn" :n 18 :gi 17 :defn "map-fn" :ns "resources/packages/core/hof/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/core/hof/impls.clj" :line 11 :lang "clj" :mins 1.6 :head "(defbase map-fn [func coll]" :see (("executor/arg-builder" . "arg-builder")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A HOF base-fn as the executor sees it: `func` arrives already `hof-wrap`ped (the slot's type resolves to `:fn`, so the executor hands over a callable, never a value) and the impl is one library call. The comment is the lesson — the seq is realised with `doall` INSIDE the execution scope, because a lazy seq escaping the effect-gate / trace / cancel bindings gets realised later, ungated and mislabeled. Streaming stays on the transducer arity.")
    (:key "executor/fix-fn" :n 19 :gi 18 :defn "fix-fn" :ns "resources/packages/core/recursion/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/core/recursion/impls.clj" :line 20 :lang "clj" :mins 2.5 :head "(defbase fix-fn" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Recursion. A bare graph cycle through `ref-fn-id` is rejected at write time (twice — per binding, and at sync's topological sort), so a fn cannot name itself. `:fix` is the ONE door: `step` receives `{:input … :self …}` and re-enters through `:self`, depth-capped by `*max-recursion-depth*`. docs/RECURSION.md records the road not taken (lazy ref resolution) and why this combinator won.")
    (:key "executor/hof-lambda-params" :n 20 :gi 19 :defn "hof-lambda-params" :ns "graphden.executor.compile.renames" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile/renames.clj" :line 1007 :lang "clj" :mins 7.4 :head "(defn hof-lambda-params" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("executor/build-ref-renames" . "build-ref-renames") ("executor/compute-rename-aliases" . "compute-rename-aliases"))
     :say "Which of a callback's free args are the per-element PARAMETERS and which are captured from the caller (docs/CLOSURE_CAPTURE.md). Resolution order: an authored `:lambda-params` on the fn-def (`[]` = capture everything — handler chains), else the slot's structural `[:fn {ARGS} RET]` shape, accepted only when unambiguous. Everything `hof-wrap` does at call time was decided here at compile time.")
    (:key "executor/build-ref-renames" :n 21 :gi 20 :defn "build-ref-renames" :ns "graphden.executor.compile.renames" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile/renames.clj" :line 1116 :lang "clj" :mins 4.1 :head "(defn build-ref-renames" :see (("crud/public-free-entries" . "public-free-entries")) :refs (("packages/build-rename-slot-records" . "build-rename-slot-records")) :siblings (("executor/hof-lambda-params" . "hof-lambda-params") ("executor/compute-rename-aliases" . "compute-rename-aliases"))
     :say "Renames, call-site half. When F renames a slot that its ref R still exposes under the original name (`{:as …}`, or a rename-view slot with `source-slot-id`), the caller supplies the value under F's name and R reads its own — this builds the `{R-name → F-name}` translation applied to the free-arg map on the way into R. docs/adr/ADR-inherited-rename-surface.md: public names are the closest-chain rename, applied at the BOUNDARY; the walkers stay per-fid.")
    (:key "executor/compute-rename-aliases" :n 22 :gi 21 :defn "compute-rename-aliases" :ns "graphden.executor.compile.renames" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile/renames.clj" :line 1200 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn compute-rename-aliases" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("executor/hof-lambda-params" . "hof-lambda-params") ("executor/build-ref-renames" . "build-ref-renames"))
     :say "Renames, inherited half. An own rename slot whose source chain reaches OUTSIDE the root's slot set yields one alias per chain link, so an inner walker reading `:branch-row` finds the value the caller passed as `:item`. Compile-time table, runtime copy — the worked example in the docstring is the one to trace by hand. This 1300-line namespace is the densest in the executor; these two entry points are its map.")))
  (:id "storage" :title "Storage & the graph" :status "toured" :mins 62.0 :summary "How the graph is stored and traversed — Postgres, recursive CTEs,
             the StorageCRUD / ExecutionGraph protocols."
   :steps
   ((:key "storage/Storage" :n 1 :gi 22 :defn "Storage" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.core" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/core.clj" :line 81 :lang "clj" :mins 1.1 :head "(defprotocol Storage" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("storage/StorageCRUD" . "StorageCRUD") ("storage/ExecutionGraph" . "ExecutionGraph"))
     :say "The storage layer starts with three protocols; this is the first. `Storage` is the lifecycle contract (`initialize` / `close`) every backend implements, binding a concrete store to a `DataSchema` before any graph can be read or written.")
    (:key "storage/StorageCRUD" :n 2 :gi 23 :defn "StorageCRUD" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.core" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/core.clj" :line 117 :lang "clj" :mins 3.3 :head "(defprotocol StorageCRUD" :see nil :refs (("storage/resolve-execution-graph" . "resolve-execution-graph") ("platform/RemoteStorage" . "RemoteStorage")) :siblings (("storage/Storage" . "Storage") ("storage/ExecutionGraph" . "ExecutionGraph"))
     :say "The row-level contract: `read-entity`, `query-entities`, `create-entity`, and friends. Every graph read the executor does bottoms out in these calls — learn this shape and you know how the whole system touches the database.")
    (:key "storage/ExecutionGraph" :n 3 :gi 24 :defn "ExecutionGraph" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.core" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/core.clj" :line 258 :lang "clj" :mins 0.9 :head "(defprotocol ExecutionGraph" :see (("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!")) :refs nil :siblings (("storage/Storage" . "Storage") ("storage/StorageCRUD" . "StorageCRUD"))
     :say "One method: `resolve-execution-graph` — given a root fn-id, return its entire reachable subgraph in a single shot. This is the seam the executor's `rebuild!` reads through, and it is implemented twice below: a backend-agnostic version and a Postgres-optimized one.")
    (:key "storage/DataSchema" :n 4 :gi 25 :defn "DataSchema" :ns "graphden.schema.protocol.protocol" :file "src/graphden/schema/protocol/protocol.clj" :line 28 :lang "clj" :mins 5.8 :head "(defprotocol DataSchema" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "What a schema exposes: the entities, their fields, and the field types (`:ref`, `:enum`, `:union`). Rows are shaped by this vocabulary before storage ever sees them — so read it before the row-storage code below.")
    (:key "storage/MalliDataSchema" :n 5 :gi 26 :defn "MalliDataSchema" :ns "graphden.schema.malli.core" :file "src/graphden/schema/malli/core.clj" :line 12 :lang "clj" :mins 2.4 :head "(defrecord MalliDataSchema" :see (("boot/postgres" . "postgres")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "…and its ONE implementation. The record is an immutable bag of maps (`entities-map`, `enums-map`, `entity-uuids-map`, `retired-fields-map`) with every protocol method a lookup into them — which is the point: a schema is data a builder assembled, not code. `create-builder` at the bottom of the file is what the `:db/schema` component calls, and the `extend-builder` fns below are how each subsystem contributes its entities to it.")
    (:key "storage/extend-builder" :n 6 :gi 27 :defn "extend-builder" :ns "graphden.schema.graph.schema" :file "src/graphden/schema/graph/schema.clj" :line 651 :lang "clj" :mins 11.7 :head "(defn extend-builder" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Where the graph model is actually declared: the `ns` / `fn` / `slot` / `binding` / `binding-list-item` entities and the `:ref` edges between them. This is the concrete table + foreign-key layout that every traversal walks.

Long, but it is one `->` pipeline rather than an algorithm: an `add-entity` per table with its fields inline, the `add-constraint` calls that become real UNIQUE indexes, and at the end the `retire-field` entries that keep dropped columns (`binding.rename-to`, `fn.impl-hash`) from being resurrected. Skim it entity by entity — this is DDL that happens to be data.")
    (:key "storage/valid-type?" :n 7 :gi 28 :defn "valid-type?" :ns "graphden.schema.fields.types" :file "src/graphden/schema/fields/types.clj" :line 160 :lang "clj" :mins 1.1 :head "(defn valid-type?" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The runtime link between a field-spec's declared type and an actual value: one predicate per abstract type, permissive on unknown types. `storage/protocol/crud_validation` runs this on every write — the live value-validation path (the old parallel malli validate-entity pipeline was dead code and is gone).")
    (:key "storage/ExecutionGraphResult" :n 8 :gi 29 :defn "ExecutionGraphResult" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/graph.clj" :line 159 :lang "clj" :mins 0.7 :head "(defrecord ExecutionGraphResult" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("storage/->execution-graph" . "->execution-graph") ("storage/process-fn-node" . "process-fn-node") ("storage/resolve-execution-graph-bfs" . "resolve-execution-graph-bfs"))
     :say "The record every resolver returns: the entity collections plus by-fn / by-binding indexes. Know this shape first and the two resolvers below read easily — they all just fill it in.")
    (:key "storage/->execution-graph" :n 9 :gi 30 :defn "->execution-graph" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/graph.clj" :line 169 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(defn ->execution-graph" :see nil :refs (("storage/resolve-execution-graph~2" . "resolve-execution-graph")) :siblings (("storage/ExecutionGraphResult" . "ExecutionGraphResult") ("storage/process-fn-node" . "process-fn-node") ("storage/resolve-execution-graph-bfs" . "resolve-execution-graph-bfs"))
     :say "Builds that record and its O(1) convenience indexes — the shared `assemble the result` step both backends funnel into.")
    (:key "storage/process-fn-node" :n 10 :gi 31 :defn "process-fn-node" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/graph.clj" :line 272 :lang "clj" :mins 2.2 :head "(defn process-fn-node" :see nil :refs (("storage/reachable-fns-sql" . "reachable-fns-sql")) :siblings (("storage/ExecutionGraphResult" . "ExecutionGraphResult") ("storage/->execution-graph" . "->execution-graph") ("storage/resolve-execution-graph-bfs" . "resolve-execution-graph-bfs"))
     :say "Expands one fn during traversal, extracting its outgoing edges: parent-ids, base / element / return-type, and binding + list-item `ref-fn-id`s. This function IS the authoritative definition of what counts as a graph edge.")
    (:key "storage/resolve-execution-graph-bfs" :n 11 :gi 32 :defn "resolve-execution-graph-bfs" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/graph.clj" :line 299 :lang "clj" :mins 2.3 :head "(defn resolve-execution-graph-bfs" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("storage/ExecutionGraphResult" . "ExecutionGraphResult") ("storage/->execution-graph" . "->execution-graph") ("storage/process-fn-node" . "process-fn-node"))
     :say "The backend-agnostic BFS driver: keep calling `process-fn-node` until the reachable closure is exhausted. This is the reference algorithm the Postgres recursive CTE mirrors.")
    (:key "storage/resolve-execution-graph" :n 12 :gi 33 :defn "resolve-execution-graph" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.generic-graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/generic_graph.clj" :line 36 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn resolve-execution-graph" :see (("storage/StorageCRUD" . "StorageCRUD")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The default `ExecutionGraph` implementation — it wires the `StorageCRUD` loaders into the BFS. Correct on any backend, but it costs ~4N round-trips, which is exactly what motivates the Postgres specialization next.")
    (:key "storage/PostgresStorage" :n 13 :gi 34 :defn "PostgresStorage" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.core" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/core.clj" :line 134 :lang "clj" :mins 10.3 :head "(defrecord PostgresStorage" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The concrete Postgres backend. Its `ExecutionGraph` extension delegates to the Postgres resolver instead of the generic BFS — showing how a backend overrides the default path when it can do better.

Its length is protocol SURFACE, not logic: one record implementing `Storage`, `StorageIntrospection`, `StorageCRUD`, the constraint protocols and `ExecutionGraph` in turn, most methods a single call deep. Read the protocol headings on the way down and stop at the `ExecutionGraph` arm — it is the only one doing something the generic path does not.")
    (:key "storage/do-initialize" :n 14 :gi 35 :defn "do-initialize" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.migration" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/migration.clj" :line 297 :lang "clj" :mins 3.2 :head "(defn do-initialize" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Nobody hand-writes DDL here: the schema of the previous steps is DATA, and this diffs it against the metadata table to emit exactly the changes needed — first-init or migration, all in one transaction. Read the docstring for the fleet property that makes it safe: a transaction-scoped advisory lock taken BEFORE the state read, so N pods booting against one database serialize, and the second one computes a no-op instead of re-planning against pre-DDL state. This is why a graphden upgrade needs no DB reset (docs/OPERATIONS.md).")
    (:key "storage/reachable-fns-sql" :n 15 :gi 36 :defn "reachable-fns-sql" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/graph.clj" :line 44 :lang "clj" :mins 4.5 :head "(def ^:private reachable-fns-sql" :see (("storage/process-fn-node" . "process-fn-node")) :refs nil :siblings (("storage/resolve-execution-graph~2" . "resolve-execution-graph"))
     :say "The `WITH RECURSIVE` CTE that walks every outgoing edge from the seed fn in one statement and returns the reachable `fn.id` set. This is the heart of the recursive-CTE traversal, mirroring in SQL exactly the edge list that `process-fn-node` computes in Clojure.")
    (:key "storage/resolve-execution-graph~2" :n 16 :gi 37 :defn "resolve-execution-graph" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.graph" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/graph.clj" :line 118 :lang "clj" :mins 4.7 :head "(defn resolve-execution-graph" :see (("storage/->execution-graph" . "->execution-graph")) :refs nil :siblings (("storage/reachable-fns-sql" . "reachable-fns-sql"))
     :say "The optimized resolver: run the CTE to get reachable ids, enforce the graph-size cap, bulk-load fn / slot / binding rows with five `IN`-clause queries, and hand off to `->execution-graph`. O(1) round-trips versus the generic O(N) — the payoff of the whole protocol split.")
    (:key "storage/make-emitter" :n 17 :gi 38 :defn "make-emitter" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.notify" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/notify.clj" :line 304 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defn make-emitter" :see (("crud/notify-after-write!" . "notify-after-write!")) :refs nil :siblings (("storage/create-listener" . "create-listener"))
     :say "How a committed write reaches every OTHER pod. The emitter is a closure over the pooled DataSource that sends one `pg_notify` on `graphden_events`; best-effort by design — a NOTIFY failure must never roll back a row-level write, because the mutation-driven reconcile is the correctness path and NOTIFY only makes propagation fast. `notify-after-write!` in the CRUD block is its caller.")
    (:key "storage/create-listener" :n 18 :gi 39 :defn "create-listener" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.notify" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/notify.clj" :line 244 :lang "clj" :mins 2.4 :head "(defn create-listener" :see (("versioning/dispatch*" . "dispatch*") ("services/reconcile-once!" . "reconcile-once!")) :refs nil :siblings (("storage/make-emitter" . "make-emitter"))
     :say "The receiving end: one DEDICATED connection (outside the pool — LISTEN is session state) subscribed to `graphden_events`, a background poll thread, reconnect with backoff, and a callback set that `register!` / `unregister!` mutate. Callback exceptions are isolated so one bad listener cannot silence the rest. The branch router's invalidation, the reconciler and the SSE panels all hang off this handle.")))
  (:id "versioning" :title "Branches & versioning" :status "toured" :mins 102.6 :summary "The VersionedStorage decorator, per-branch resolution, diff/merge
             and branch-local? propagation."
   :steps
   ((:key "versioning/wrap-with-versioning" :n 1 :gi 40 :defn "wrap-with-versioning" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/core.clj" :line 1242 :lang "clj" :mins 2.2 :head "(defn wrap-with-versioning" :see nil :refs (("crud/resolve-branch-ref" . "resolve-branch-ref") ("boot/postgres" . "postgres")) :siblings (("versioning/VersionedStorage" . "VersionedStorage") ("versioning/create-entity-versioned!" . "create-entity-versioned!") ("versioning/purgeable-identity-ids" . "purgeable-identity-ids"))
     :say "The entry point: take any base storage, ensure a `main` branch exists, and return a `VersionedStorage` bound to it. Start here to see how the entire versioning layer is bolted onto the plain storage from the previous block.")
    (:key "versioning/VersionedStorage" :n 2 :gi 41 :defn "VersionedStorage" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/core.clj" :line 990 :lang "clj" :mins 12.6 :head "(defrecord VersionedStorage" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/wrap-with-versioning" . "wrap-with-versioning") ("versioning/create-entity-versioned!" . "create-entity-versioned!") ("versioning/purgeable-identity-ids" . "purgeable-identity-ids"))
     :say "The decorator record itself. It implements every storage protocol, delegating non-versioned entities straight to the base storage and intercepting versioned ones — reads resolve to a branch view, writes append version rows. Every method is a two-line dispatch: read this for the SHAPE, then follow the versioned arm into its own function. Grasp this structure before anything else here.")
    (:key "versioning/create-entity-versioned!" :n 3 :gi 42 :defn "create-entity-versioned!" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/core.clj" :line 436 :lang "clj" :mins 7.2 :head "(defn- create-entity-versioned!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/wrap-with-versioning" . "wrap-with-versioning") ("versioning/VersionedStorage" . "VersionedStorage") ("versioning/purgeable-identity-ids" . "purgeable-identity-ids"))
     :say "The versioned arm of a single create, and the write path worth reading in full: identity row + version row, the identity-field diff for when the deterministic id already exists, and the advisory-lock branch that serialises the writes whose uniqueness is a per-branch RESOLVED-VIEW property (fn names, list-item positions) rather than a table constraint. `update-entity-versioned!` next to it tells the same story for the append case.")
    (:key "versioning/entity-config" :n 4 :gi 43 :defn "entity-config" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.resolution" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/resolution.clj" :line 142 :lang "clj" :mins 3.1 :head "(def entity-config" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/resolve-version" . "resolve-version") ("versioning/collect-branch-chain" . "collect-branch-chain") ("versioning/resolve-version-from-cache" . "resolve-version-from-cache"))
     :say "The authoritative map of WHICH entities are versioned (fn / fn-slot / binding / binding-list-item) and how each splits across two tables: `version-data-fields` live in the version table, identity fields stay on the base row. Everything downstream keys off this config.")
    (:key "versioning/derive-version-fields" :n 5 :gi 44 :defn "derive-version-fields" :ns "graphden.schema.versioned.schema" :file "src/graphden/schema/versioned/schema.clj" :line 559 :lang "clj" :mins 4.1 :head "(defn derive-version-fields" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Where the `-version` mirror entities come from: derived from the base entity's field map (refs demote to bare uuids, framework columns spliced in), never hand-copied. A new base column without a pinned mirror uuid or an identity-level declaration fails the build loudly — the old failure mode was the decorator silently dropping that column's writes. `version-data-fields` above derives from the same source, so the two can never disagree.")
    (:key "versioning/resolve-version" :n 6 :gi 45 :defn "resolve-version" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.resolution" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/resolution.clj" :line 279 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defn resolve-version" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/entity-config" . "entity-config") ("versioning/collect-branch-chain" . "collect-branch-chain") ("versioning/resolve-version-from-cache" . "resolve-version-from-cache"))
     :say "The public per-branch read: for one entity, load its version chain, merge in a single batched pass, and return the winning version (nil on a tombstone). This is how a plain read becomes branch-aware.")
    (:key "versioning/collect-branch-chain" :n 7 :gi 46 :defn "collect-branch-chain" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.resolution" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/resolution.clj" :line 461 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn collect-branch-chain" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/entity-config" . "entity-config") ("versioning/resolve-version" . "resolve-version") ("versioning/resolve-version-from-cache" . "resolve-version-from-cache"))
     :say "The branch-chain walk: `[branch → parent → … → root]` via `:base-branch-id`, memoised process-wide. This inheritance backbone is what every resolve and cache-invalidation consults to know which branches a read can see.")
    (:key "versioning/resolve-version-from-cache" :n 8 :gi 47 :defn "resolve-version-from-cache" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.resolution" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/resolution.clj" :line 591 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn- resolve-version-from-cache" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/entity-config" . "entity-config") ("versioning/resolve-version" . "resolve-version") ("versioning/collect-branch-chain" . "collect-branch-chain"))
     :say "The in-memory chain walk the resolver actually runs: at each branch level combine own-latest with merge candidates, pick the greatest `effective-ts`, and recurse to the parent. One tested algorithm shared by both single-entity and whole-graph reads.")
    (:key "versioning/effective-branch-local?" :n 9 :gi 48 :defn "effective-branch-local?" :ns "graphden.versioning.branch-local" :file "src/graphden/versioning/branch_local.clj" :line 109 :lang "clj" :mins 1.6 :head "(defn effective-branch-local?" :see nil :refs (("layout/annotate" . "annotate")) :siblings nil
     :say "Monotonic-OR over the `:parent-ids` closure: a fn (or ANY ancestor) marked `:branch-local? true` is local forever. The resolver's merge filter calls this so runtime-config fns — web-server port, vault path, cron schedule — never leak across branches on merge.")
    (:key "versioning/check-list-item-position-collisions!" :n 10 :gi 49 :defn "check-list-item-position-collisions!" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.uniqueness" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/uniqueness.clj" :line 25 :lang "clj" :mins 6.7 :head "(defn check-list-item-position-collisions!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/check-resource-override-path-collision!" . "check-resource-override-path-collision!"))
     :say "The list-item position-collision guard, now in a shared `uniqueness` namespace so BOTH the write path (`core`) and the merge path (`merge`) enforce it without a require cycle. A cross-branch base index on `(binding-id, position)` would wrongly block divergence, so per-branch uniqueness is enforced here in the resolved view instead — a subtle invariant the write path defends with an advisory lock. Fn names follow the same doctrine: `check-fn-name-collision!` just below replaced the raw `UNIQUE (namespace_id, name)`, whose soft-deleted identities blocked their names forever.")
    (:key "versioning/check-resource-override-path-collision!" :n 11 :gi 50 :defn "check-resource-override-path-collision!" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.uniqueness" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/uniqueness.clj" :line 169 :lang "clj" :mins 2.9 :head "(defn check-resource-override-path-collision!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/check-list-item-position-collisions!" . "check-list-item-position-collisions!"))
     :say "The same resolved-view doctrine applied to the newest versioned entity: `:resource-override` (UI Step 1) shadows a shipped frontend asset — path → content, per branch — so the editor's own JS/CSS is editable from inside the editor (Operate → Assets). One override per path per branch view, guarded here; serving goes through `:read-resource-overridable` in the app package, and every save rolls the effective `?v=` asset hash so browsers re-fetch.")
    (:key "versioning/purgeable-identity-ids" :n 12 :gi 51 :defn "purgeable-identity-ids" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/core.clj" :line 202 :lang "clj" :mins 3.8 :head "(defn- purgeable-identity-ids" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/wrap-with-versioning" . "wrap-with-versioning") ("versioning/VersionedStorage" . "VersionedStorage") ("versioning/create-entity-versioned!" . "create-entity-versioned!"))
     :say "Delete has TWO modes, and this guard is the heart of the hard one. User-facing deletes tombstone (a version row with `:deleted-at`, so descendants hide the entity too); sync + rollback hard-delete. A hard delete used to drop only the branch's version rows — the surviving versionless identity became a GHOST that a later re-mint of the same deterministic id revived through the update path, content-equal, invisible on every list read (the 2026-07-20 route-vanish incident). Now the identity row goes too, but ONLY when no other branch retains a version and no child identity still references it — per-branch isolation stays intact.")
    (:key "versioning/repoint-refs!" :n 13 :gi 52 :defn "repoint-refs!" :ns "graphden.versioning.identity-repair" :file "src/graphden/versioning/identity_repair.clj" :line 52 :lang "clj" :mins 3.8 :head "(defn repoint-refs!" :see nil :refs (("packages/sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" . "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!")) :siblings nil
     :say "The identity-plane repair primitives for the OTHER ghost source: a package fn's id is `uuid-v5(ns-path, name)`, so a namespace move mints a new id and abandons the old row with every ref still aimed at it. `repoint-refs!` fills refs in place across BOTH planes (identity and every branch's version rows); `purge-fn-subgraph!` below then removes the abandoned subgraph. The sync-time reconciler (`packages.sync/reconcile-moved-identities!`) runs them after every package sync, and the operator tool `graphden.dev.integrity` delegates to the same two. Its REMOVAL side asks `inbound-refs-many` with `:live-only?` — only the newest version of a row on a live branch counts as a ref, never the create-time identity row or a superseded version — because those two kept every retired chain a live fn had ever referenced pinned forever (472 leftovers on a two-month-old instance, 2026-09-04).")
    (:key "versioning/diff-branches" :n 14 :gi 53 :defn "diff-branches" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.merge" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/merge.clj" :line 403 :lang "clj" :mins 4.4 :head "(defn diff-branches" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/detect-conflicts" . "detect-conflicts") ("versioning/merge-branch!" . "merge-branch!"))
     :say "The branch-symmetric resolved-view diff: gather every entity touched on either side's visibility set, resolve both, and report added/modified. The simplest read-only comparison — it precedes the conflict-oriented framing below.")
    (:key "versioning/diff-branches-view" :n 15 :gi 54 :defn "diff-branches-view" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.diff-view" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/diff_view.clj" :line 203 :lang "clj" :mins 6.5 :head "(defn diff-branches-view" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The DISPLAY model over diff-branches (diff v2): entries grouped under the fn that owns them (list-items chain through their binding), per-field before/after pairs for modified rows, and batch name-resolution for slots and fn-typed refs. The editor's compare mode (inspector diff panel + Review dialog) client-renders THIS shape from the :diff-branches-view base-fn's JSON endpoint; the v1 API keeps the flat wire shape.")
    (:key "versioning/detect-conflicts" :n 16 :gi 55 :defn "detect-conflicts" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.merge" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/merge.clj" :line 183 :lang "clj" :mins 3.7 :head "(defn detect-conflicts" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/diff-branches" . "diff-branches") ("versioning/merge-branch!" . "merge-branch!"))
     :say "Finds entities modified on BOTH branches after the computed fork point (skipping branch-local fns). This introduces fork-point reasoning and defines exactly what a merge must resolve before it can proceed.")
    (:key "versioning/merge-branch!" :n 17 :gi 56 :defn "merge-branch!" :ns "graphden.versioning.storage.merge" :file "src/graphden/versioning/storage/merge.clj" :line 657 :lang "clj" :mins 8.9 :head "(defn merge-branch!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/diff-branches" . "diff-branches") ("versioning/detect-conflicts" . "detect-conflicts"))
     :say "The merge itself: assert conflicts are resolved, then atomically write ONE `branch-merge` record — which makes the source's versions visible through resolution, copying no rows — plus any resolution overrides. The payoff step tying resolution and conflict detection together.

Read it in three parts: validate (conflicts resolved, approval policy satisfied), write — the single `branch-merge` row plus any resolution overrides, in one transaction — then report, including `skipped-as-branch-local` right below it: the rows a merge deliberately does NOT carry across.")
    (:key "versioning/merge-transitively!" :n 18 :gi 57 :defn "merge-transitively!" :ns "graphden.versioning.merge.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/merge/core.clj" :line 263 :lang "clj" :mins 4.4 :head "(defn merge-transitively!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/validate-approval-policy!" . "validate-approval-policy!") ("versioning/branch-content-stamp" . "branch-content-stamp"))
     :say "What makes a merge transitive: the storage layer surfaces only the source's OWN rows and refuses when the source inherits from a branch the target lacks, so this layer merges those branches in first — root-most first, each through the same policy gate a direct merge would face — then the source, and reports the steps as `:merged-first`. docs/VERSIONING.md § Known gaps.")
    (:key "versioning/validate-approval-policy!" :n 19 :gi 58 :defn "validate-approval-policy!" :ns "graphden.versioning.merge.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/merge/core.clj" :line 212 :lang "clj" :mins 3.1 :head "(defn validate-approval-policy!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/merge-transitively!" . "merge-transitively!") ("versioning/branch-content-stamp" . "branch-content-stamp"))
     :say "Merge GOVERNANCE — the code-review gate. When the target branch sets `:required-approvals`, a merge into it is refused (`:branch/approval-required`, 409) until the proposal has enough VALID approvals: distinct, non-stale, BOUND to this exact merge target (`target-branch-id`), and — when the target names a restrictive `:approver-ids` — from that allow-list. Counting is pure/open-core (WHO may approve was enforced when each `:branch-approval` row was written, and re-checked here so a tightened policy or a wrong-target approval can't slip through); the live merge base-fn calls this right after the `:forbid-invalid?` gate. This is how 'protect main, land via reviewed merges' is enforced.")
    (:key "versioning/branch-content-stamp" :n 20 :gi 59 :defn "branch-content-stamp" :ns "graphden.versioning.merge.core" :file "src/graphden/versioning/merge/core.clj" :line 141 :lang "clj" :mins 1.6 :head "(defn branch-content-stamp" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/merge-transitively!" . "merge-transitively!") ("versioning/validate-approval-policy!" . "validate-approval-policy!"))
     :say "The stale-dismissal fingerprint: a branch's own version rows reduced to `count|max(created-at)`. An approval records the source's stamp at approval time; any later edit APPENDS a version row, so the stamp advances and `count-valid-approvals` stops counting the now- stale approval — GitHub's 'dismiss stale reviews on a new push', expressed as a monotonic content signal rather than an event hook.")
    (:key "versioning/bump!" :n 21 :gi 60 :defn "bump!" :ns "graphden.storage.postgres.graph-epoch" :file "src/graphden/storage/postgres/graph_epoch.clj" :line 97 :lang "clj" :mins 2.4 :head "(defn bump!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The graph EPOCH — freshness as a comparison, not an imperative. Every graph-shaped write bumps this sequence BEFORE writing; the router's fetch-time check (`validate-graph-epoch!`) heals any cached ctx whose eager invalidation was skipped — a client abort mid-request, a write path with no NOTIFY, a lost NOTIFY. The eager delta machinery and NOTIFY remain pure latency optimizations, which is what their docstrings always claimed.")
    (:key "versioning/build-branch-ctx" :n 22 :gi 61 :defn "build-branch-ctx" :ns "graphden.system.branch-router" :file "src/graphden/system/branch_router.clj" :line 134 :lang "clj" :mins 4.3 :head "(defn- build-branch-ctx" :see (("executor/create-context" . "create-context")) :refs nil :siblings (("versioning/dispatch*" . "dispatch*"))
     :say "Builds a fresh live `ExecutionContext` whose storage is a `VersionedStorage` rewrapped at the requested branch, with its own compiled-registry and graph-cache atoms — and (2026-08-31) its own RICH-TYPES SLICE, forked from the base's view at build time and bound as the registry override for the ctx's compiles and every request dispatched to it. This is how a branch gets its own executor without re-sharding the graph.")
    (:key "versioning/dispatch*" :n 23 :gi 62 :defn "dispatch*" :ns "graphden.system.branch-router" :file "src/graphden/system/branch_router.clj" :line 1435 :lang "clj" :mins 8.6 :head "(defn- dispatch*" :see nil :refs (("storage/create-listener" . "create-listener") ("web/dispatch-to-branch" . "dispatch-to-branch")) :siblings (("versioning/build-branch-ctx" . "build-branch-ctx"))
     :say "The top-level Ring dispatch: extract the branch ref (header or query), resolve it to a branch-id, and route the request to that branch's lazily-built handler (400 on an unknown branch). Where a live request finally lands in the correct per-branch context. Its public wrapper `dispatch` first short-circuits `/livez` — the one registry-independent liveness path — so a pod busy on a full recompile still answers its probe.")))
  (:id "types" :title "Type system" :status "toured" :mins 82.5 :summary "Subtyping, unification, narrowing, refinements, the sweep-at-zero
             checker, and the :secret information-flow marker."
   :steps
   ((:key "types/primitives" :n 1 :gi 63 :defn "primitives" :ns "graphden.types.core.shapes" :file "src/graphden/types/core/shapes.clj" :line 23 :lang "clj" :mins 2.8 :head "(def primitives" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/child-types" . "child-types") ("types/taint-with-secret-if-tainted" . "taint-with-secret-if-tainted"))
     :say "Start with the leaf vocabulary. This is the flat enum of primitive value-kinds plus the special `:any` (top) and `:never` (bottom). Every type in the system is built from these, so nothing structural makes sense until you have them.")
    (:key "types/child-types" :n 2 :gi 64 :defn "child-types" :ns "graphden.types.core.shapes" :file "src/graphden/types/core/shapes.clj" :line 417 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(defn child-types" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/primitives" . "primitives") ("types/taint-with-secret-if-tainted" . "taint-with-secret-if-tainted"))
     :say "The type grammar in one function: every compound arm — record maps, `[:list]`, `[:map]`, `[:tuple]`, `[:refine]`, `[:secret]`, `[:union]`, `[:fn]` — and how each recurses. This doubles as the map of how records / unions / variants / fn-types are represented.")
    (:key "types/well-formed?" :n 3 :gi 65 :defn "well-formed?" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 153 :lang "clj" :mins 2.9 :head "(defn well-formed?" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/make-union" . "make-union") ("types/desugar-variant" . "desugar-variant") ("types/subtype?" . "subtype?") ("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/unify" . "unify") ("types/strip-null" . "strip-null"))
     :say "The syntactic validity guard at the boundary between user input (fns.edn / the API) and the rest of the module. Reading it cements the exact shape of each type kind: fn args-map, effects set, refine base, secret inner.")
    (:key "types/make-union" :n 4 :gi 66 :defn "make-union" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 230 :lang "clj" :mins 5.4 :head "(defn make-union" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/well-formed?" . "well-formed?") ("types/desugar-variant" . "desugar-variant") ("types/subtype?" . "subtype?") ("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/unify" . "unify") ("types/strip-null" . "strip-null"))
     :say "The smart constructor for `[:union …]`: flatten, dedupe, absorb `:never`, and drop any member subsumed by another. This canonical union shape is what the rest of the system relies on — and your introduction to sum types.")
    (:key "types/desugar-variant" :n 5 :gi 67 :defn "desugar-variant" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 329 :lang "clj" :mins 2.5 :head "(defn desugar-variant" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/well-formed?" . "well-formed?") ("types/make-union" . "make-union") ("types/subtype?" . "subtype?") ("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/unify" . "unify") ("types/strip-null" . "strip-null"))
     :say "Rewrites `[:variant tag T …]` into a structural union of tag-pinned records. Shows how the higher-level variant sugar reduces to the union + refinement machinery you just met — no new runtime concept needed.")
    (:key "types/subtype?" :n 6 :gi 68 :defn "subtype?" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 1224 :lang "clj" :mins 13.7 :head "(defn subtype?" :see nil :refs (("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/taint-with-secret-if-tainted" . "taint-with-secret-if-tainted")) :siblings (("types/well-formed?" . "well-formed?") ("types/make-union" . "make-union") ("types/desugar-variant" . "desugar-variant") ("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/unify" . "unify") ("types/strip-null" . "strip-null"))
     :say "The heart of assignability: reflexive / transitive subtyping across primitives, `:jsonb` sinks, records, lists, maps, tuples, refinements, unions, secrets, and fn-types. Every checker decision — is `actual` a subtype of `expected`? — bottoms out here.

It is one `cond` ladder and the ORDER is the semantics: the universal yes/no arms first (identity, `:any`, `:never`, the typevar → `:jsonb` escape), then the `:empty-map` sentinel, unions (EVERY member on the left, SOME member on the right), primitives, `:jsonb`, refinements, the structural shapes, and the `:secret` marker last. Don't hold the whole ladder in your head — find the arm for the pair of shapes you are asking about and read the comment that argues for it.")
    (:key "types/constraint-implies?" :n 7 :gi 69 :defn "constraint-implies?" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 1160 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn constraint-implies?" :see (("types/subtype?" . "subtype?")) :refs nil :siblings (("types/well-formed?" . "well-formed?") ("types/make-union" . "make-union") ("types/desugar-variant" . "desugar-variant") ("types/subtype?" . "subtype?") ("types/unify" . "unify") ("types/strip-null" . "strip-null"))
     :say "Decides when one refinement constraint entails another (numeric bounds, `:in` / `:=` / `:not=`, `:and` / `:or`). This is exactly how `subtype?` compares refinement types like `:positive-int` ⊆ `:non-negative-int`, so read it right after.")
    (:key "types/unify" :n 8 :gi 70 :defn "unify" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 1652 :lang "clj" :mins 10.1 :head "(defn unify" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/well-formed?" . "well-formed?") ("types/make-union" . "make-union") ("types/desugar-variant" . "desugar-variant") ("types/subtype?" . "subtype?") ("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/strip-null" . "strip-null"))
     :say "Robinson unification with occurs-check and subtype-aware leniency, plus a union fallback. This is how polymorphic type variables get bound during checking — the two-way complement to the one-way `subtype?` test.

Same shape as `subtype?`, so read it the same way: one `cond` over the normalised pair — typevar arms bind first (with the occurs check), then structural recursion into fn / list / record shapes, then the union fallback. It returns a substitution map or the `::fail` sentinel that `unify-ok?` / `unify-fail?` just below test for.")
    (:key "types/strip-null" :n 9 :gi 71 :defn "strip-null" :ns "graphden.types.core" :file "src/graphden/types/core.clj" :line 306 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn strip-null" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/well-formed?" . "well-formed?") ("types/make-union" . "make-union") ("types/desugar-variant" . "desugar-variant") ("types/subtype?" . "subtype?") ("types/constraint-implies?" . "constraint-implies?") ("types/unify" . "unify"))
     :say "The single source of truth for nullability narrowing: remove `:null` from a top-level union (bare `:null` → `:never`) while NEVER stripping a `:secret`. The canonical example of type narrowing, shared by control-flow refinement and the `:coalesce` rule.")
    (:key "types/taint-with-secret-if-tainted" :n 10 :gi 72 :defn "taint-with-secret-if-tainted" :ns "graphden.types.core.shapes" :file "src/graphden/types/core/shapes.clj" :line 485 :lang "clj" :mins 3.2 :head "(defn taint-with-secret-if-tainted" :see (("types/subtype?" . "subtype?")) :refs (("crud/create-secret" . "create-secret")) :siblings (("types/primitives" . "primitives") ("types/child-types" . "child-types"))
     :say "The `:return-type-rule` propagator that wraps a fn's return in `[:secret …]` when any argument carries a secret anywhere. This is how the information-flow marker flows through composition — the payoff of the secret arm you saw in `subtype?`.")
    (:key "types/classify-literal" :n 11 :gi 73 :defn "classify-literal" :ns "graphden.types.check.literals" :file "src/graphden/types/check/literals.clj" :line 39 :lang "clj" :mins 3.4 :head "(defn classify-literal" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Infers a structural type from a literal Clojure value (records, header maps, `[:list T]`, primitives). This is where concrete argument values enter the checker as types — the input side of every binding check.")
    (:key "types/check-binding!" :n 12 :gi 74 :defn "check-binding!" :ns "graphden.types.check" :file "src/graphden/types/check.clj" :line 490 :lang "clj" :mins 6.0 :head "(defn- check-binding!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/signature-return" . "signature-return") ("types/check-fn-def!" . "check-fn-def!") ("types/check-all-defs!" . "check-all-defs!"))
     :say "Verifies one binding's `actual` ⊆ `expected`, running `unify` when type-vars are present and handling the `:any` / `:never` / refinement-on-literal escape hatches. The atomic unit of type-checking, tying together `subtype?`, `unify`, and `classify-literal`.")
    (:key "types/signature-return" :n 13 :gi 75 :defn "signature-return" :ns "graphden.types.check" :file "src/graphden/types/check.clj" :line 2135 :lang "clj" :mins 4.0 :head "(defn signature-return" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/check-binding!" . "check-binding!") ("types/check-fn-def!" . "check-fn-def!") ("types/check-all-defs!" . "check-all-defs!"))
     :say "The declared-signature engine: when a root base-fn has no hand `:return-type-rule`, the polymorphic declaration itself computes the return — unify each var-carrying declared arg against the actual type at the site, resolve the declared return through the substitution. `:coll [:list a] → [:union :null a]` IS the rule; the hand-written rules that survive are only those reading literal values, union fan-out, or the registry.")
    (:key "types/check-fn-def!" :n 14 :gi 76 :defn "check-fn-def!" :ns "graphden.types.check" :file "src/graphden/types/check.clj" :line 2418 :lang "clj" :mins 8.3 :head "(defn check-fn-def!" :see nil :refs (("crud/type-check-fn-after-mutation!" . "type-check-fn-after-mutation!")) :siblings (("types/check-binding!" . "check-binding!") ("types/signature-return" . "signature-return") ("types/check-all-defs!" . "check-all-defs!"))
     :say "Type-checks a single fn-def against its (single or multiple- inheritance) parents: resolve parent slots, run the monotonicity / unknown-slot / effect guards, unify bindings, compute the return via type-rules, propagate effects, and snapshot the rich-type into the registry. The per-node checker the sweep repeats.")
    (:key "types/check-all-defs!" :n 15 :gi 77 :defn "check-all-defs!" :ns "graphden.types.check" :file "src/graphden/types/check.clj" :line 2538 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn check-all-defs!" :see nil :refs (("packages/sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" . "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!")) :siblings (("types/check-binding!" . "check-binding!") ("types/signature-return" . "signature-return") ("types/check-fn-def!" . "check-fn-def!"))
     :say "The whole-graph sweep entry point: fold `check-fn-def!` over topologically-ordered fn-defs so each def's computed rich-type is registered before its dependents read it. This is how the entire graph is checked end to end.")
    (:key "types/record-rich-types!" :n 16 :gi 78 :defn "record-rich-types!" :ns "graphden.executor.registry.core" :file "src/graphden/executor/registry/core.clj" :line 438 :lang "clj" :mins 10.4 :head "(defn record-rich-types!" :see (("executor/compile-all" . "compile-all") ("web/api-rich-types" . "api-rich-types")) :refs nil :siblings (("types/rich-type-of-id" . "rich-type-of-id"))
     :say "Where the sweep's answers are KEPT. The rich-types registry is the in-memory `{fn → {:return :args :effects …}}` map every downstream consumer reads: the compiler's HOF-wrap decision, the executor's effect gate, secret redaction on persist, the editor's type chips. BRANCH-SCOPED since 2026-08-31: writes land on the ACTIVE atom — the global for the default branch, a per-ctx fork for every other (bound by the branch router at build + dispatch), so `/api/types` answers per branch instead of last-compile-wins. Aliases resolve to their structural body here, so everyone downstream sees one canonical shape.")
    (:key "types/rich-type-of-id" :n 17 :gi 79 :defn "rich-type-of-id" :ns "graphden.executor.registry.core" :file "src/graphden/executor/registry/core.clj" :line 646 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn rich-type-of-id" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("types/record-rich-types!" . "record-rich-types!"))
     :say "…and how it is READ — by fn **id**, not name. Names are per-namespace labels and may repeat; ids cannot (docs/adr/ADR-identity-model.md, enforced in CI by `id_resolution_guard_test`). The `:by-name` index survives only for base-fns and legacy call sites. Before you key any new registry or dispatch by name, read the ADR — this is the mistake it exists to stop.")))
