     :say "Runs after ordering to check declared semver constraints against the classpath, failing fast at boot with `:packages/version-conflict` instead of a cryptic `Unknown parent` later. The version machinery guarding load order.")
    (:key "packages/load-single-package" :n 5 :gi 107 :defn "load-single-package" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 469 :lang "clj" :mins 2.4 :head "(defn- load-single-package" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/process-module" . "process-module") ("packages/base-fn?" . "base-fn?") ("packages/load-module-fns" . "load-module-fns") ("packages/load-impls-via-eval" . "load-impls-via-eval") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def") ("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!"))
     :say "For one package: read its `package.edn` metadata and reduce over its `:modules`, merging each module's base-fn-defs and fn-defs. The bridge from `a package` down to `its modules`.")
    (:key "packages/process-module" :n 6 :gi 108 :defn "process-module" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 410 :lang "clj" :mins 3.7 :head "(defn- process-module" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/load-single-package" . "load-single-package") ("packages/base-fn?" . "base-fn?") ("packages/load-module-fns" . "load-module-fns") ("packages/load-impls-via-eval" . "load-impls-via-eval") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def") ("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!"))
     :say "The heart of one module: load its `fns.edn` and `impls.clj`, then `group-by` splits the entries into base-fns (which need impls) versus fn-defs (compositions / type-rows). Where fns.edn and impls.clj first meet.")
    (:key "packages/base-fn?" :n 7 :gi 109 :defn "base-fn?" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 303 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn- base-fn?" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/load-single-package" . "load-single-package") ("packages/process-module" . "process-module") ("packages/load-module-fns" . "load-module-fns") ("packages/load-impls-via-eval" . "load-impls-via-eval") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def") ("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!"))
     :say "The classifier `process-module` groups on: an entry is a base-fn only if it has no `:parent` / `:parents` and is not a type-row. This is the fundamental base-fn versus fn-def distinction a newcomer must internalize.")
    (:key "packages/load-module-fns" :n 8 :gi 110 :defn "load-module-fns" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 152 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn- load-module-fns" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/load-single-package" . "load-single-package") ("packages/process-module" . "process-module") ("packages/base-fn?" . "base-fn?") ("packages/load-impls-via-eval" . "load-impls-via-eval") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def") ("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!"))
     :say "Reads a module's `fns.edn` (pure EDN data) via a source-position reader, attaching `:source-file` / `:source-line` to every fn-def so later type errors point at `file:line`. How the on-disk declarations enter memory.")
    (:key "packages/parse-module" :n 9 :gi 111 :defn "parse-module" :ns "graphden.packages.records.parse" :file "src/graphden/packages/records/parse.clj" :line 1234 :lang "clj" :mins 4.5 :head "(defn parse-module" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/parse-composed" . "parse-composed"))
     :say "Where an `fns.edn` entry becomes ROWS. Two passes: mint the deterministic ids (`name->id`, so the same def yields the same uuid on every sync), then `parse-fn-def` each entry with the whole module — plus prior syncs' defs — in hand, because resolving which ancestor a bound arg targets needs to see the ancestors' shapes.")
    (:key "packages/parse-composed" :n 10 :gi 112 :defn "parse-composed" :ns "graphden.packages.records.parse" :file "src/graphden/packages/records/parse.clj" :line 713 :lang "clj" :mins 6.7 :head "(defn- parse-composed" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/parse-module" . "parse-module"))
     :say "The composed case: `:parent` / `:parents` become the parent-ids junction, each `:args` entry becomes a binding row on the inherited slot it targets — or, when the entry is `{:type T …}` with no binding markers, a NEW own slot on top of inheritance. Which of the two, and on which ancestor, is `resolve-slot-owner`'s call.")
    (:key "packages/resolve-slot-owner" :n 11 :gi 113 :defn "resolve-slot-owner" :ns "graphden.packages.records.slot-resolution" :file "src/graphden/packages/records/slot_resolution.clj" :line 460 :lang "clj" :mins 6.8 :head "(defn resolve-slot-owner" :see (("executor/build-lookups" . "build-lookups")) :refs nil :siblings (("packages/build-rename-slot-records" . "build-rename-slot-records"))
     :say "Given a composed fn and an arg name, find the ancestor that DECLARED the slot — walking the inheritance chain, then the refs' trees, and disambiguating a name owned on both paths by type. This is the parse-time twin of the executor's `build-lookups`: the same slot identity, computed once from EDN so the binding row carries the right `slot-id` before a single row is written.")
    (:key "packages/build-rename-slot-records" :n 12 :gi 114 :defn "build-rename-slot-records" :ns "graphden.packages.records.slot-resolution" :file "src/graphden/packages/records/slot_resolution.clj" :line 652 :lang "clj" :mins 5.2 :head "(defn build-rename-slot-records" :see (("executor/build-ref-renames" . "build-ref-renames")) :refs nil :siblings (("packages/resolve-slot-owner" . "resolve-slot-owner"))
     :say "A `{Y {:as :X}}` rename is not a binding column — it is a NEW slot owned by the renaming fn, linked to its source through `source-slot-id`. That FK is what `rename-for-slot` and the editor's `getEffectiveSlotName` read, and what the executor's rename translation walks. Minted here, on parse.")
    (:key "packages/load-impls-via-eval" :n 13 :gi 115 :defn "load-impls-via-eval" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 211 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn- load-impls-via-eval" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/load-single-package" . "load-single-package") ("packages/process-module" . "process-module") ("packages/base-fn?" . "base-fn?") ("packages/load-module-fns" . "load-module-fns") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def") ("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!"))
     :say "The counterpart to fns.edn: slurp `impls.clj`, `eval` each form in a fresh namespace, and return that namespace's `impls` var — the `{fn-name → impl-fn}` map. How Clojure code on disk becomes live functions. The `reuse-loaded-impls?` arm is why coverage can see this layer at all: evaluating from the resource leaves cloverage with nothing to instrument, so under `bb coverage` the already- instrumented namespace is reused instead of re-evaluated.")
    (:key "packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" :n 14 :gi 116 :defn "fn-def->base-fn-def" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 327 :lang "clj" :mins 4.7 :head "(defn- fn-def->base-fn-def" :see (("executor/resolve-impl" . "resolve-impl")) :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/load-single-package" . "load-single-package") ("packages/process-module" . "process-module") ("packages/base-fn?" . "base-fn?") ("packages/load-module-fns" . "load-module-fns") ("packages/load-impls-via-eval" . "load-impls-via-eval") ("packages/sync-namespaces!" . "sync-namespaces!"))
     :say "The glue: marry an fns.edn entry (args, return-type, effects, tags) with its impl-map value into the registry-shaped `{:args … :return-type … :impl fn …}`. Literally where fns.edn and impls.clj fuse into a base-fn.")
    (:key "packages/add" :n 15 :gi 117 :defn "add" :ns "resources/packages/core/arithmetic/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/core/arithmetic/impls.clj" :line 53 :lang "clj" :mins 0.7 :head "(defbase add [nums]" :see (("executor/defbase" . "defbase")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A concrete `defbase` base-fn impl — `(defbase add [nums] (safe-arith + nums :add))`. This is the value `load-impls-via-eval` collects and `fn-def->base-fn-def` pairs with the `:add` entry in fns.edn. Grounds the abstract loader in a real arithmetic primitive.")
    (:key "packages/sync-namespaces!" :n 16 :gi 118 :defn "sync-namespaces!" :ns "graphden.packages.loader" :file "src/graphden/packages/loader.clj" :line 668 :lang "clj" :mins 4.3 :head "(defn sync-namespaces!" :see (("executor/sync-fns-to-storage!" . "sync-fns-to-storage!")) :refs nil :siblings (("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages") ("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/resolve-dependencies" . "resolve-dependencies") ("packages/validate-dep-constraints!" . "validate-dep-constraints!") ("packages/load-single-package" . "load-single-package") ("packages/process-module" . "process-module") ("packages/base-fn?" . "base-fn?") ("packages/load-module-fns" . "load-module-fns") ("packages/load-impls-via-eval" . "load-impls-via-eval") ("packages/fn-def->base-fn-def" . "fn-def->base-fn-def"))
     :say "Closes the loop into storage: take the collected namespace paths, build the `core` → `core.arithmetic` parent-child `:ns` entity hierarchy, and return `{ns-path → id}` that the fn-def sync hangs the live fn rows under.")
    (:key "packages/sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" :n 17 :gi 119 :defn "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" :ns "graphden.packages.sync" :file "src/graphden/packages/sync.clj" :line 918 :lang "clj" :mins 11.4 :head "(defn sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" :see (("types/check-all-defs!" . "check-all-defs!") ("versioning/repoint-refs!" . "repoint-refs!")) :refs (("boot/packages" . "packages")) :siblings (("packages/reconcile-bundle-scope!" . "reconcile-bundle-scope!"))
     :say "The other half of every boot, and the one the loader hands off to: sync composed fn-defs to storage, heal namespace-moved identities and reap retired ones nothing live references (`_anon-*` shapes included; `{:dry-run? true}` previews the plan from a REPL), seed each fn-def's rich-type, then run the topological type-check sweep. Read the two long comments — the batch-size binding (a fresh-DB boot writes >10k fn rows past the guard meant for USER writes) and `:skip-type-check?` (~15s of sweep the test bootstrap may opt out of, production never does — which is precisely why `bb type-sweep` exists: EVERY fixture opts out, so before that check a corpus type regression surfaced only when a real instance booted). This is declarative sync: the packages on disk are the desired state, storage is reconciled toward it.")
    (:key "packages/records->fn-defs" :n 18 :gi 120 :defn "records->fn-defs" :ns "graphden.packages.export" :file "src/graphden/packages/export.clj" :line 564 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn records->fn-defs" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/ref-kw" . "ref-kw") ("packages/external-deps" . "external-deps") ("packages/strip-secret-paths" . "strip-secret-paths"))
     :say "The loader's INVERSE: typed graph records back to fns.edn-shaped fn-def maps. Everything distribution-shaped (publish, install, whole-graph download) rides this, and its contract is a fixpoint — `parse(export(parse(x))) == parse(x)` — pinned by the corpus-fixpoint test over the real core/web/app packages. Read it as `process-module` run backwards.")
    (:key "packages/ref-kw" :n 19 :gi 121 :defn "ref-kw" :ns "graphden.packages.export" :file "src/graphden/packages/export.clj" :line 108 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defn- ref-kw" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/records->fn-defs" . "records->fn-defs") ("packages/external-deps" . "external-deps") ("packages/strip-secret-paths" . "strip-secret-paths"))
     :say "How a ref serializes: BARE keyword when the name is unique (canonical minimal form), namespace-QUALIFIED when it's duplicated across the export set. Qualification never falls back to bare — a version-materialized `@`-ns qualifies as `(keyword \"lib@1-2-0.sub\" n)` and the root ns as `(keyword \"\" n)`, both legal in-memory values even though no EDN reader could parse their printed form.")
    (:key "packages/encode-unreadable-kws" :n 20 :gi 122 :defn "encode-unreadable-kws" :ns "graphden.packages.records.wire" :file "src/graphden/packages/records/wire.clj" :line 70 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defn encode-unreadable-kws" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The EDN-TEXT boundary is the one place those unspellable keywords break, so exactly there they become `#graphden/ref \"lib@…/n\"` tagged literals before `pr-str`; `wire-readers` decodes them back at the package loader and the remote bundle reader. In-memory and JSONB paths keep raw keywords — the storage codec round-trips them.")
    (:key "packages/external-deps" :n 21 :gi 123 :defn "external-deps" :ns "graphden.packages.export" :file "src/graphden/packages/export.clj" :line 886 :lang "clj" :mins 5.1 :head "(defn external-deps" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/records->fn-defs" . "records->fn-defs") ("packages/ref-kw" . "ref-kw") ("packages/strip-secret-paths" . "strip-secret-paths"))
     :say "Scoped export: a namespace SUBTREE becomes a publishable bundle — fns under the root are the package's content, and this analysis pass turns refs pointing OUT of the subtree into its declared `:dependencies` (what install must already have) plus the `:package-dependencies` those refs' `@`-versioned namespaces map to. The bundle ASSEMBLY around it is graph composition — the `:export-namespace` fn-def in registry/registry/fns.edn. This is the unit the in-graph registry versions.")
    (:key "packages/strip-secret-paths" :n 22 :gi 124 :defn "strip-secret-paths" :ns "graphden.packages.export" :file "src/graphden/packages/export.clj" :line 742 :lang "clj" :mins 2.0 :head "(defn strip-secret-paths" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/records->fn-defs" . "records->fn-defs") ("packages/ref-kw" . "ref-kw") ("packages/external-deps" . "external-deps"))
     :say "The share-safety policy: vault paths are STRIPPED from bundles by default (the slot stays a free `[:secret :text]` arg) and the `:secrets` manifest lists what was affected — the publisher sees the warning, the installer gets a `:needs-definition` todo. A bundle is deliberately not a full copy of your secrets.")
    (:key "packages/publish-package-apply" :n 23 :gi 125 :defn "publish-package-apply" :ns "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :line 218 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defbase publish-package-apply" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/upsert-pin!" . "upsert-pin!") ("packages/import-bundle!" . "import-bundle!"))
     :say "Publish lands the bundle IN the graph: an immutable, content-hashed `:package-version` row (name + semver + fns + declared deps). Install is its mirror — `materialize-package-fns` syncs the pinned version's fns into a version-qualified `@`-namespace and writes a `:package-install` pin; update/rollback rewrites refs between versions; fork copies copy-on-write with no pin.")
    (:key "packages/upsert-pin!" :n 24 :gi 126 :defn "upsert-pin!" :ns "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :line 427 :lang "clj" :mins 2.6 :head "(defn- upsert-pin!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/publish-package-apply" . "publish-package-apply") ("packages/import-bundle!" . "import-bundle!"))
     :say "Where the marketplace's install COUNT comes from (docs/MARKETPLACE.md). A new pin is an install and bumps the global `:package-stat` row with an atomic `INSERT … ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` over the pool — deliberately inside the pin write unit, like the invalidation an entity write owes, because a graph-side 'was it pinned?' probe is an effectful read a later ref would re-run AFTER the pin. Moving a pin (update / rollback) is not an install. Everything else the marketplace needs — listing normalisation, the cards' rating aggregate, the filters, the partials, the review upsert, the storefront bodies — is graph, four modules sharing the `app.marketplace` namespace: `registry/marketplace{,-surface, -storefront,-moderation}/fns.edn`.")
    (:key "packages/moderate-package-version!" :n 25 :gi 127 :defn "moderate-package-version!" :ns "resources/packages/registry/marketplace/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/registry/marketplace/impls.clj" :line 76 :lang "clj" :mins 3.4 :head "(defbase moderate-package-version!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The operator's decision on a public listing (docs/MARKETPLACE.md § 8). Three things to notice: the platform-admin gate sits at the deepest effectful core so no route bypasses it; the read goes through `registry-shared/platform-base` — the storage BENEATH the org-scoped decorator, because the operator's own org would hide another org's pending row; and the updated row is raised through core's notification seam (`tc/notify! :package-moderated`), which the tenancy addon turns into the publisher's email. The module's impls file is small on purpose: identity, roster, semver, the origin card and moderation — everything else is graph.")
    (:key "packages/install!" :n 26 :gi 128 :defn "install!" :ns "graphden.packages.loaded" :file "src/graphden/packages/loaded.clj" :line 45 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn install!" :see nil :refs (("constellation/extra-deps" . "extra-deps")) :siblings nil
     :say "The loaded-package ROSTER — recorded by `load-packages` itself, so boot and the test golden bootstrap leave the same read model. One row per package (version, description, base-fn count, `impl+fns` vs `fns-only`, `bundled` vs `manifest`); the marketplace's Executor tab lists it through `:loaded-packages`. It is the honest answer to 'what does this executor run?' without offering to install a Type-2 package, which is code and enters through the build.")
    (:key "packages/import-bundle!" :n 27 :gi 129 :defn "import-bundle!" :ns "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/registry/registry/impls.clj" :line 788 :lang "clj" :mins 5.2 :head "(defbase import-bundle!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/publish-package-apply" . "publish-package-apply") ("packages/upsert-pin!" . "upsert-pin!"))
     :say "The WRITE half of the export/import pair — `POST /api/import/graph` applies an exported bundle to a NAMED branch: resolve/create the target (owner-stamped, `owner` write-policy — the push-branch convention), skip package-owned defs, adopt editor-born same-name identities onto their deterministic ids, sync through the SAME path the boot loader uses, optionally prune to snapshot semantics, delta-invalidate that branch. Cross-install push/pull and git-snapshot restores all land here, then ride the normal diff → review → merge flow.")
    (:key "packages/reconcile-bundle-scope!" :n 28 :gi 130 :defn "reconcile-bundle-scope!" :ns "graphden.packages.sync" :file "src/graphden/packages/sync.clj" :line 461 :lang "clj" :mins 4.6 :head "(defn reconcile-bundle-scope!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("packages/sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" . "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!"))
     :say "Snapshot semantics for a runtime import (`?prune=true`): within exactly the bundle's namespaces, a deterministic-id fn the bundle no longer contains is TOMBSTONED on the branch (a `:deleted-at` version — so it hides an INHERITED fn too, not just a branch-local one, and the deletion propagates on merge). Never the boot reconciler's identity-plane purge, which would reach across branches. Referenced removals are kept + reported, loud.")
    (:key "packages/bundle->files" :n 29 :gi 131 :defn "bundle->files" :ns "graphden.packages.git-format" :file "src/graphden/packages/git_format.clj" :line 163 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defn bundle->files" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The GIT face: a bundle laid out as one module file per namespace (the fns.edn module shape) + a manifest, byte-deterministic so a git diff is a graph diff. Read the seq-order printer note — the parser derives slot POSITIONS from map iteration order, so the printer must not 'canonicalise' nested keys. `graphden.cli` (export/import/push/pull) is the HTTP-only round-trip over this.")
    (:key "packages/lint" :n 30 :gi 132 :defn "lint" :ns "graphden.lint.core" :file "src/graphden/lint/core.clj" :line 741 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(defn lint" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The graph's own linter, run over the same loader output at CI time (`bb graph-lint`): duplicate definitions — exact, and after inlining `_`-private helpers — dead privates (unreferenced, and the cluster behind them), bindings that restate what a fn inherits, siblings that should share a parent, and over-deep chains. One engine over EDN-shape fn-defs, so the editor's per-branch graph is linted by the same code. docs/GRAPH_LINT.md.")
    (:key "packages/lint-branch" :n 31 :gi 133 :defn "lint-branch" :ns "graphden.lint.graph" :file "src/graphden/lint/graph.clj" :line 324 :lang "clj" :mins 2.3 :head "(defn lint-branch" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The editor's side of the same engine: the per-ctx graph snapshot (org-sliced, branch-resolved) is rebuilt into loader-shaped fn-defs and linted, memoised per branch while that branch's snapshot object is unchanged. The ⚐ lens and the Inspector's Lint section share this one call; the suppressions it subtracts live in the graph as a `:const`.")))
  (:id "boot" :title "Boot & lifecycle" :status "toured" :mins 23.7 :summary "How the process starts: the Integrant component graph, the Aero
             config + addon manifest, and the router seams installed at boot."
   :steps
   ((:key "boot/-main" :n 1 :gi 134 :defn "-main" :ns "graphden.executor-runtime.core" :file "src/graphden/executor_runtime/core.clj" :line 187 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn -main" :see (("boot/boot!" . "boot!") ("services/reconcile-once!" . "reconcile-once!")) :refs nil :siblings (("boot/boot!" . "boot!") ("constellation/-main" . "-main"))
     :say "**Where the JVM starts.** Four lines that say what a graphden process IS: boot the Integrant system, install a shutdown hook, optionally open an nREPL (`GRAPHDEN_NREPL_PORT` — the door the `clojure` MCP tools come through), then park forever. Nothing here serves HTTP; the web server is a `:service` row the reconciler starts, several components down.")
    (:key "boot/boot!" :n 2 :gi 135 :defn "boot!" :ns "graphden.executor-runtime.core" :file "src/graphden/executor_runtime/core.clj" :line 167 :lang "clj" :mins 2.0 :head "(defn boot!" :see nil :refs (("boot/-main" . "-main") ("constellation/-main" . "-main")) :siblings (("boot/-main" . "-main") ("constellation/-main" . "-main"))
     :say "Start, or HALT the partial system and exit non-zero. Integrant does NOT auto-halt when an init-key throws, and several components have already spawned non-daemon threads by then — without this the JVM stays half-alive with no shutdown hook and `/health` can pass on a boot that actually failed. The partial system rides in the ex-info's `:system` key. Read the docstring: it is the incident this function exists for.")
    (:key "boot/read-config" :n 3 :gi 136 :defn "read-config" :ns "graphden.system.config" :file "src/graphden/system/config.clj" :line 83 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn read-config" :see (("platform/platform-tier?" . "platform-tier?")) :refs (("constellation/addon-config-resources" . "addon-config-resources") ("constellation/-main" . "-main")) :siblings (("constellation/addon-config-resources" . "addon-config-resources") ("constellation/merge-addon" . "merge-addon"))
     :say "Configuration is data: Aero reads `system-<profile>.edn` (`:dev` / `:test` / `:prod`) with `#ig/ref` and `#var` reader tags registered above, then deep-merges any ADDON config fragments named in `GRAPHDEN_ADDON_CONFIGS`. That merge is the open-core seam (docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md § Addon config manifest) — the private tenancy addon REDEFINES core keys and ADDS its own without a single edit to this repo. With no addons the merge is a no-op.")
    (:key "boot/start!" :n 4 :gi 137 :defn "start!" :ns "graphden.system.interface" :file "src/graphden/system/interface.clj" :line 38 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn start!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "`(ig/init (read-config profile))` — that is the whole system lifecycle. The component graph and its dependency edges are documented in `graphden.system.core`'s docstring, which exists only to `:require` the seven `init.*` namespaces for their `defmethod` side effects, so every `ig/init-key` is registered before this call. The next four steps walk that graph in dependency order.")
    (:key "boot/postgres" :n 5 :gi 138 :defn "postgres" :ns "graphden.system.init.storage" :file "src/graphden/system/init/storage.clj" :line 102 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defmethod ig/init-key :db/postgres [_ {:keys [datasource-wrap] :as opts}]" :see (("versioning/wrap-with-versioning" . "wrap-with-versioning")) :refs (("storage/MalliDataSchema" . "MalliDataSchema")) :siblings nil
     :say "The first component with a socket (`:db/schema` before it is pure — it is the `create-builder` + `extend-builder` chain from the storage block): open the Postgres pool, run `initialize-with-cleanup!` against that schema, seed traits. Note `:datasource-wrap` — a `DataSource → DataSource` fn the tenancy addon supplies so every connection carries `graphden.current_org` for row-level security. Absent in core: a plain pool. `:db/versioned` wraps this one in the versioning decorator.")
    (:key "boot/packages" :n 6 :gi 139 :defn "packages" :ns "graphden.system.init.packages" :file "src/graphden/system/init/packages.clj" :line 18 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defmethod ig/init-key :app/packages [_ {:keys [package-names extra-package-names]}]" :see (("packages/load-packages" . "load-packages") ("packages/sync-fn-entities-from-packages!" . "sync-fn-entities-from-packages!")) :refs (("constellation/read-manifest" . "read-manifest")) :siblings nil
     :say "Load the package set — core + web + app, plus the addon's `:extra-package-names` (the fns-channel seam) and any external Type-2 packages from the operator manifest. Its two siblings just below, `:exec/base-fns` and `:exec/fn-entities`, are thin shells over `graphden.packages.sync` — this is where **the graph gets its content** on every boot.")
    (:key "boot/context" :n 7 :gi 140 :defn "context" :ns "graphden.system.init.exec" :file "src/graphden/system/init/exec.clj" :line 125 :lang "clj" :mins 7.7 :head "(defmethod ig/init-key :exec/context" :see (("executor/create-context" . "create-context")) :refs (("constellation/merge-addon" . "merge-addon")) :siblings (("boot/compiled-registry" . "compiled-registry"))
     :say "Assembles the one `ExecutionContext` the whole process runs against, and it reads as a list of the seams core exposes: auth-provider, request-scope, execute-guard, vault client, notify-emitter, fleet forward-hop, and `:compile-storage` — the privileged org-agnostic handle `rebuild!` reads the graph STRUCTURE through, while data reads stay org-scoped. In single-tenant every one of these is the default and the `cond->` tail is a no-op.")
    (:key "boot/compiled-registry" :n 8 :gi 141 :defn "compiled-registry" :ns "graphden.system.init.exec" :file "src/graphden/system/init/exec.clj" :line 240 :lang "clj" :mins 1.0 :head "(defmethod ig/init-key :exec/compiled-registry [_ {:keys [context]}]" :see (("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!") ("executor/registry" . "registry")) :refs nil :siblings (("boot/context" . "context"))
     :say "Two lines with a ~50s cost: compile every fn in the graph into a closure BEFORE the first request. This is the eager half of the executor's compile-once story — after this key, `registry` in the hot path is a warm holder read, never a cold compile.")
    (:key "boot/dispatch-first" :n 9 :gi 142 :defn "dispatch-first" :ns "graphden.system.route-collection" :file "src/graphden/system/route_collection.clj" :line 89 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn dispatch-first" :see nil :refs (("web/sync-fn-defs-branch!" . "sync-fn-defs-branch!") ("constellation/merge-addon" . "merge-addon")) :siblings nil
     :say "The last boot-time seam. Optional subsystems (the `registry` package, `/mcp`) and the tenancy addon each install a `:router-or-nil` Ring callable here under a distinct key; the core handler chain tries them in turn and falls through to the app router on `nil`. With nothing installed the collection is `{}`, this short-circuits, and single-tenant behaviour is byte-for-byte unchanged — the shape every seam in this codebase aims for.")
    (:key "boot/check-router!" :n 10 :gi 143 :defn "check-router!" :ns "graphden.system.api-url-drift" :file "src/graphden/system/api_url_drift.clj" :line 236 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defn check-router!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A boot-time guard rather than a component: enumerate the router's paths, scan every editor JS module for `/api/*` string literals, and refuse to start if one names a route the router does not serve. This is why the frontend never hardcodes a path the backend can silently move — the coupling is checked once, at boot, on the real router.")
    (:key "boot/-main~2" :n 11 :gi 144 :defn "-main" :ns "graphden.cli" :file "src/graphden/cli.clj" :line 304 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn -main" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The same jar has a second entry point. `graphden.cli` moves whole branches between INSTANCES over the export/import HTTP API — `export` / `import` / `diff`, and `push` / `pull` against a hub — with nothing but a URL and a token. It is how a self-hosted graph reaches a cloud org and back, and the model for any other out-of-process tool: the API is the contract, not the DB.")
    (:key "boot/register-checkpoint-hooks!" :n 12 :gi 145 :defn "register-checkpoint-hooks!" :ns "graphden.crac" :file "src/graphden/crac.clj" :line 202 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn register-checkpoint-hooks!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "CRaC (checkpoint/restore) is the optional fast-start track (docs/FLEET_RFC.md): snapshot the warmed JVM, restore in milliseconds. The catch is that CRIU cannot snapshot a socket, so `quiesce!` drains the Hikari pool, closes the LISTEN and advisory-lock connections and the http-kit server — schedulers paused FIRST so no tick reopens one mid-checkpoint — and `resume!` undoes it. Inert on a plain JVM; `development/crac/` has the bake.")))
  (:id "web" :title "Web — routing & the page runtime" :status "toured" :mins 26.5 :summary "HTTP from socket to response body: routing, middleware as graph
             fns, the hiccup page runtime, and the APIs the editor reads."
   :steps
   ((:key "web/http-server" :n 1 :gi 146 :defn "http-server" :ns "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :line 239 :lang "clj" :mins 3.7 :head "(defbase http-server" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/realize-request-body" . "realize-request-body") ("web/pick-encoding-fn" . "pick-encoding-fn"))
     :say "The http-kit wrapper base-fn: `run-server` spins the listener thread and binds the composed Ring handler to a port, recording `:network` / `:process` effects. Where a request first enters the web layer, so the natural first stop. Note it is a tiny primitive — everything else is composed from small base-fns in `web/http/fns.edn`.")
    (:key "web/realize-request-body" :n 2 :gi 147 :defn "realize-request-body" :ns "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :line 137 :lang "clj" :mins 0.7 :head "(defbase realize-request-body" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/http-server" . "http-server") ("web/pick-encoding-fn" . "pick-encoding-fn"))
     :say "Fully realizes the incoming request body so downstream graph handlers see plain data instead of a one-shot stream — the very next thing that happens to a raw request after it lands.")
    (:key "web/ring-handler-fn" :n 3 :gi 148 :defn "ring-handler-fn" :ns "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :line 46 :lang "clj" :mins 0.8 :head "(defbase ring-handler-fn" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/ring-router-fn" . "ring-router-fn") ("web/ring-create-default-handler-fn" . "ring-create-default-handler-fn") ("web/middleware" . "middleware"))
     :say "Composes the compiled reitit router with the default handler into the single Ring callable http-kit actually invokes per request. The seam between the server socket and routing.")
    (:key "web/ring-router-fn" :n 4 :gi 149 :defn "ring-router-fn" :ns "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :line 22 :lang "clj" :mins 1.1 :head "(defbase ring-router-fn" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/ring-handler-fn" . "ring-handler-fn") ("web/ring-create-default-handler-fn" . "ring-create-default-handler-fn") ("web/middleware" . "middleware"))
     :say "Builds the bare `reitit.ring/router` from route data — which is itself graph-composed vectors and keywords. This is the routing table the handler consults to match a path to a handler.")
    (:key "web/ring-create-default-handler-fn" :n 5 :gi 150 :defn "ring-create-default-handler-fn" :ns "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :line 33 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defbase ring-create-default-handler-fn" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/ring-handler-fn" . "ring-handler-fn") ("web/ring-router-fn" . "ring-router-fn") ("web/middleware" . "middleware"))
     :say "Constructs the fallback handler reitit uses when nothing matches, wrapping the graph-composed 404 / 405 / 406 response maps. The `no route` branch.")
    (:key "web/middleware" :n 6 :gi 151 :defn "middleware" :ns "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/reitit/impls.clj" :line 64 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defbase middleware" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/ring-handler-fn" . "ring-handler-fn") ("web/ring-router-fn" . "ring-router-fn") ("web/ring-create-default-handler-fn" . "ring-create-default-handler-fn"))
     :say "The middleware factory: turn a graph `body` (with `:request` and `:next-handler` free args) into a reitit `{:name :wrap}` spec folded around each route handler. How cross-cutting logic — like the branch-routing wrap next — is expressed as a graph fn.")
    (:key "web/current-branch-router" :n 7 :gi 152 :defn "current-branch-router" :ns "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :line 10 :lang "clj" :mins 0.9 :head "(defbase current-branch-router" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/dispatch-to-branch" . "dispatch-to-branch"))
     :say "Reads the currently-installed per-branch router from a global atom (nil before boot installs it). The branch-routing wrap needs this router first.")
    (:key "web/dispatch-to-branch" :n 8 :gi 153 :defn "dispatch-to-branch" :ns "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/branch-router/impls.clj" :line 18 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(defbase dispatch-to-branch" :see (("versioning/dispatch*" . "dispatch*")) :refs nil :siblings (("web/current-branch-router" . "current-branch-router"))
     :say "The branch-routing wrap: delegate the Ring request to the correct per-branch handler resolved from the router above. This is how ONE server multiplexes requests across branch-scoped graphs — the package side of the versioning block's `dispatch*`.")
    (:key "web/authenticate-request" :n 9 :gi 154 :defn "authenticate-request" :ns "resources/packages/web/ring-adapter/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/ring-adapter/impls.clj" :line 12 :lang "clj" :mins 1.0 :head "(defbase authenticate-request" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The auth gate applied to a request inside the handler chain before a page or action runs. Sits between routing/dispatch and the handler body that will build a response.")
    (:key "web/write-rej" :n 10 :gi 155 :defn "write-rej" :ns "resources/packages/web/crud-write/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/crud-write/impls.clj" :line 36 :lang "clj" :mins 2.2 :head "(defbase write-rej" :see (("crud/write-rej" . "write-rej") ("crud/apply-create-core" . "apply-create-core")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The CRUD HTTP surface is one namespace, `web.crud`, spread over seven modules (`crud`, `-types`, `-write`, `-delete`, `-seq`, `-parse`, `-routes`) — every `/api/entities/*` route is a fn-def over base-fns like this one. `write-rej` is the whole of stage 2 as ONE library boundary: the cycle / MI / override / list-closed chain from the CRUD block, returned as `{:reason …}` or nil, so the parse → validate → apply pipeline is visible in the graph.")
    (:key "web/form-decode" :n 11 :gi 156 :defn "form-decode" :ns "resources/packages/web/crud-parse/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/crud-parse/impls.clj" :line 83 :lang "clj" :mins 1.7 :head "(defbase form-decode" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Stage 1 of the same pipeline, and a model base-fn: one codec call, the ring-vector-for-repeated-keys quirk collapsed so the declared `:text-map` return stays honest, and a comment explaining why no regex (a 200KB asset override would trip `*max-regex-input-length*`). The `htmx` module next door is fns-only — `hx-*` attribute builders, no impl at all.")
    (:key "web/hiccup-element" :n 12 :gi 157 :defn "hiccup-element" :ns "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :line 26 :lang "clj" :mins 1.0 :head "(defbase hiccup-element" :see (("editor/bindActionDispatch" . "bindActionDispatch") ("editor/loadPartial" . "loadPartial")) :refs (("editor/bindActionDispatch" . "bindActionDispatch")) :siblings (("web/h-raw" . "h-raw") ("web/render-hiccup" . "render-hiccup"))
     :say "The generic `[tag attrs children]` constructor behind the graph-native page runtime. User-composed pages are fn-defs (`:html-page`, components) that emit hiccup through this primitive — including the `data-action` attrs that the editor's JavaScript runtime (`resources/packages/app/editor/*.js`) binds on click — alongside the `hx-*` attrs whose server-rendered `/partials/*` fragments now carry logic that once lived in that JS. Where a user's page becomes a concrete hiccup tree.")
    (:key "web/h-raw" :n 13 :gi 158 :defn "h-raw" :ns "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :line 18 :lang "clj" :mins 1.0 :head "(defbase h-raw" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/hiccup-element" . "hiccup-element") ("web/render-hiccup" . "render-hiccup"))
     :say "Wraps a string in a hiccup `RawString` so inline script / style bodies (like the editor's bundled JS / CSS) render verbatim instead of being HTML-escaped. The escape hatch that lets a composed page embed the JS frontend without mangling it.")
    (:key "web/render-hiccup" :n 14 :gi 159 :defn "render-hiccup" :ns "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/html/impls.clj" :line 13 :lang "clj" :mins 0.7 :head "(defbase render-hiccup" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/hiccup-element" . "hiccup-element") ("web/h-raw" . "h-raw"))
     :say "Serializes the assembled hiccup tree into an HTML string, with a taint-propagation rule so rendered secrets stay redacted. The point where a graph-composed page turns into the response body.")
    (:key "web/pick-encoding-fn" :n 15 :gi 160 :defn "pick-encoding-fn" :ns "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/web/http/impls.clj" :line 159 :lang "clj" :mins 1.6 :head "(defbase pick-encoding-fn" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/http-server" . "http-server") ("web/realize-request-body" . "realize-request-body"))
     :say "The outbound finalizer is a graph composition now — `:process-response` in web/http/fns.edn stringifies headers, applies the `Connection: close` policy, and encodes the body, all as fn-defs over single-call primitives. This RFC 7231 Accept-Encoding negotiation is the one self-contained parse the pipeline keeps in Clojure; the DECISION to encode (thresholds, compressible pattern) lives in the graph. The last stop as the rendered page leaves the web layer back through http-kit.")
    (:key "web/compatible-type-names" :n 16 :gi 161 :defn "compatible-type-names" :ns "graphden.crud.types-api" :file "src/graphden/crud/types_api.clj" :line 183 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defn compatible-type-names" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("web/api-rich-types" . "api-rich-types"))
     :say "The server-side type-picker sweep behind `/partials/compatible-type-options`. It filters the `type-name-kinds` roster to the names that can legally narrow `expected` in ONE alias-aware `subtype?` pass — replacing the editor's former per-name fan-out of ~50 parallel `POST /api/types/compatible` requests per picker open. The graph-first migration's clearest case: a client loop collapses into one server render.")
    (:key "web/api-rich-types" :n 17 :gi 162 :defn "api-rich-types" :ns "graphden.crud.types-api" :file "src/graphden/crud/types_api.clj" :line 844 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defn api-rich-types" :see nil :refs (("types/record-rich-types!" . "record-rich-types!")) :siblings (("web/compatible-type-names" . "compatible-type-names"))
     :say "The `/api/types` wire-shaping layer the editor loads at boot and after every mutation: the augmented rich-type snapshot with `bulk-omitted-fields` stripped from each entry. The old `?fn=<name>` per-fn full-entry backfill is gone with its only client consumer — the return-type-rule popover is a server partial now, and the layout strip facts read the raw registry directly. How the type-checker's registry reaches the browser as a lean payload.")
    (:key "web/sync-fn-defs-branch!" :n 18 :gi 163 :defn "sync-fn-defs-branch!" :ns "resources/packages/mcp/mcp/impls.clj" :file "resources/packages/mcp/mcp/impls.clj" :line 30 :lang "clj" :mins 3.0 :head "(defbase sync-fn-defs-branch!" :see (("executor/sync-fns-to-storage!" . "sync-fns-to-storage!") ("boot/dispatch-first" . "dispatch-first")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The web layer has a second client: an AI. The optional `mcp` package serves `/mcp` (JSON-RPC) through the same per-branch router, and this is its only write primitive — sync fn-defs into a branch, then delta-invalidate THAT branch's registry, not the request's own (the AI writes to `ai/…` while its POST rides main). The key design point is in the docstring: writes go through the very `sync-fns-to-storage!` the package loader uses, so a proposal from an AI meets the same cycle / collision / type constraints as a human's `fns.edn`.")))
  (:id "layout" :title "Layout — the editor's graph view" :status "toured" :mins 21.7 :summary "Turn raw fn/slot/binding rows into the placed cards, edges and
             server-computed facts the editor renders."
   :steps
   ((:key "layout/load-graph-entities-uncached" :n 1 :gi 164 :defn "load-graph-entities-uncached" :ns "graphden.layout.data" :file "src/graphden/layout/data.clj" :line 324 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn load-graph-entities-uncached" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("layout/derive-fn-slot-views" . "derive-fn-slot-views"))
     :say "Stage 1's data pull — every fn / slot / fn-slot / binding / list-item row in one branch-cached batch (versioned) or five vanilla queries. The whole pipeline is a pure transform over this snapshot; a per-context graph cache keeps hover-frequency layout requests from re-reading storage.")
    (:key "layout/derive-fn-slot-views" :n 2 :gi 165 :defn "derive-fn-slot-views" :ns "graphden.layout.data" :file "src/graphden/layout/data.clj" :line 264 :lang "clj" :mins 4.3 :head "(defn derive-fn-slot-views" :see nil :refs (("editor/createFnOverlay" . "createFnOverlay")) :siblings (("layout/load-graph-entities-uncached" . "load-graph-entities-uncached"))
     :say "Synthesizes the per-fn 'arg view' rows the walkers consume: for each fn, its inherited slot surface with bindings overlaid closer-fn-wins, plus the `:source-id` chains that let an edge attribute an inherited slot to the ancestor that declared it. The slot/binding model's read-side counterpart.")
    (:key "layout/build-graph-elements" :n 3 :gi 166 :defn "build-graph-elements" :ns "graphden.layout.graph" :file "src/graphden/layout/graph.clj" :line 822 :lang "clj" :mins 5.4 :head "(defn build-graph-elements" :see nil :refs (("editor/fetchBackendLayout" . "fetchBackendLayout")) :siblings nil
     :say "Stage 2's orchestrator — dispatches the cycle-guarded `process-*` walkers over the expansion spec, then pipes the accumulated state through the post-processing transforms (optional/HOF badges, captured-edge migration, overlay dedup, strip facts). Produces the node/edge element lists the client draws verbatim.")
    (:key "layout/emit-root-deep-frees!" :n 4 :gi 167 :defn "emit-root-deep-frees!" :ns "graphden.layout.builder-helpers" :file "src/graphden/layout/builder_helpers.clj" :line 932 :lang "clj" :mins 6.4 :head "(defn emit-root-deep-frees!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "After the walkers: the root card's DEEPER holes — a template's frees behind ancestor-bound refs — as `:deepArg` placeholders on the root itself, keyed by the inner slot but naming the root fn, so the `+` binder writes a closure-capture binding where the Run form lists the hole. The entry set is the SURFACE walk (`compile.renames/deep-free-ext-entries`): it stops at HOF boundaries, so a listener does not sprout every knob of the app it serves.")
    (:key "layout/compute-layout-matrix" :n 5 :gi 168 :defn "compute-layout-matrix" :ns "graphden.layout.core" :file "src/graphden/layout/core.clj" :line 288 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(defn compute-layout-matrix" :see nil :refs (("editor/fetchBackendLayout" . "fetchBackendLayout")) :siblings nil
     :say "Stages 3+ — grid placement: roots at column zero, dependents right of their sources, rows packed to avoid overlap, plus the validation summary. Pixel geometry stays client-side (measured reflow off the real DOM); the server owns the grid topology.")
    (:key "layout/annotate" :n 6 :gi 169 :defn "annotate" :ns "graphden.layout.strip-facts" :file "src/graphden/layout/strip_facts.clj" :line 60 :lang "clj" :mins 2.3 :head "(defn annotate" :see (("versioning/effective-branch-local?" . "effective-branch-local?")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The editor-facing tail: attach server-computed FACTS to each fn-node — the inherited return-type alias, the rule-owning base-fn (`registry/rule-owner-of`), and the transitive branch-local seed (`branch-local/branch-local-seed`). These replaced three hand-written client mirrors of the same walks; the client renders facts, it doesn't re-derive them.")))
  (:id "editor" :title "Editor frontend — the browser half" :status "toured" :mins 68.0 :summary "The ~25k lines of plain ES that draw the graph, bind the edits
             and mount the server's partials. No build step, no framework."
   :steps
   ((:key "editor/initGraph" :n 1 :gi 170 :defn "initGraph" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-main.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-main.js" :line 222 :lang "js" :mins 5.5 :head "async function initGraph() {" :see (("editor/authFetch" . "authFetch")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "**Start here for anything you can click.** The editor is ~75 plain `.js` files concatenated into one bundle in a fixed order (no imports, no build step — the per-module map is in [docs/EDITOR_MODULES.md](../EDITOR_MODULES.md)), and this is the boot. Note the shape of the fetches: the namespace TREE goes out alone first, because its status answers the auth question for everyone — only a non-401 fires the three secondary loads. Fn leaves and per-fn slots/bindings arrive lazily; a 401 sends the user to sign in rather than into a red fatal banner.")
    (:key "editor/buildLookups" :n 2 :gi 171 :defn "buildLookups" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-data.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-data.js" :line 115 :lang "js" :mins 6.8 :head "function buildLookups(data) {" :see (("executor/build-lookups" . "build-lookups")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The client mirror of the compile layer's `build-lookups`: the same raw fn / slot / fn-slot / binding / list-item rows, indexed into the O(1) Maps every render path reads (`fnMap`, `slotByFnAndName`, `bindingByFnSlot`, `itemsByBinding`, the namespace-path map). Every comment here is a fixed O(N×M) scan — this function runs on every mutation, so it is where client-side graph work gets slow.")
    (:key "editor/graph" :n 3 :gi 172 :defn "graph" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-graph-model.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-graph-model.js" :line 16 :lang "js" :mins 1.3 :head "const graph = {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The graph itself: two Maps plus two adjacency indexes and an `animating` flag. This is *all* that remained of cytoscape when it was removed — the library had been reduced to a node/edge store and a position tween, so it became this file. `window.graphView` is the surface Playwright tests wait on (`ready()`, `animating()`), which is why the browser-test suite never needed rewriting.")
    (:key "editor/fetchBackendLayout" :n 4 :gi 173 :defn "fetchBackendLayout" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-layout.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-layout.js" :line 278 :lang "js" :mins 10.9 :head "async function fetchBackendLayout() {" :see (("layout/build-graph-elements" . "build-graph-elements") ("layout/compute-layout-matrix" . "compute-layout-matrix")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The client does NOT lay the graph out. It POSTs the selected root plus the per-node expansion spec to `/api/graph/layout` and gets back nodes, edges and a `nodeId → {x, y, row, col}` map. This is the wire crossing into the previous block: the server owns grid topology, the client owns pixels.

Most of its length is the sizing pass that runs AFTER the response: per-column widths, then per-column gaps computed from the widest edge label plus the type chips and badges the overlays render beside it. That is the client's one real layout responsibility — making the server's grid fit the pixels its own cards actually occupy. Read the POST at the top, then jump to the comment blocks that explain each gap term.")
    (:key "editor/renderGraph" :n 5 :gi 174 :defn "renderGraph" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-render.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-render.js" :line 98 :lang "js" :mins 9.1 :head "async function renderGraph(shouldFit = true) {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "One render cycle, and the reason it is not a one-liner: prime the selected fn's subtree, capture an ANCHOR node before the await so the canvas doesn't jump under the cursor, fetch the layout, diff it against the live graph, then re-measure — card heights are only known after the overlays exist, so the server's grid is corrected against measured DOM and the nodes settle onto the result.")
    (:key "editor/createFnOverlay" :n 6 :gi 175 :defn "createFnOverlay" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-overlay-fn.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-overlay-fn.js" :line 215 :lang "js" :mins 9.1 :head "function createFnOverlay(node, container) {" :see (("layout/derive-fn-slot-views" . "derive-fn-slot-views")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A node is not drawn — it is an absolutely-positioned HTML card riding the layer transform (edges are SVG; everything else is DOM). This builds one fn card: the inheritance levels from `parent-ids`, the use-site header when the card is an expanded child rather than the navigation root, and a row per slot. The slot/binding model of the very first block, made clickable.")
    (:key "editor/bindActionDispatch" :n 7 :gi 176 :defn "bindActionDispatch" :ns "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :file "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :line 78 :lang "js" :mins 2.3 :head "function bindActionDispatch(host) {" :see (("web/hiccup-element" . "hiccup-element")) :refs (("web/hiccup-element" . "hiccup-element")) :siblings (("editor/loadPartial" . "loadPartial"))
     :say "The pivot from client logic to server-rendered HTML. ONE delegated listener per host resolves `data-action=\"…\"` against a registry filled by `registerActionHandler`, so a fragment the server just rendered becomes interactive with no per-fragment wiring. This file is platform-shared: it is bundled into the editor AND served standalone at `/assets/graphden-runtime.js`, so user-composed pages get the same dispatch. Read the bind-once comment — without it a reused singleton popover fires `delete-fn` N times on the Nth open.")
    (:key "editor/loadPartial" :n 8 :gi 177 :defn "loadPartial" :ns "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :file "resources/packages/web/runtime/graphden-runtime.js" :line 123 :lang "js" :mins 2.6 :head "async function loadPartial(host, url, opts) {" :see nil :refs (("web/hiccup-element" . "hiccup-element")) :siblings (("editor/bindActionDispatch" . "bindActionDispatch"))
     :say "Fetch an HTML partial, swap it into a host, re-bind the dispatcher. That is the whole `/partials/*` client contract ([docs/PARTIALS.md](../PARTIALS.md)) — the migration path by which popovers that used to be hand-built DOM became graph-native fn-defs. The supersession token is the subtle part: two concurrent loads into one host must let the LATEST call win, not the fastest response.")
    (:key "editor/loadRowActionsContent" :n 9 :gi 178 :defn "loadRowActionsContent" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-row-actions.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-row-actions.js" :line 418 :lang "js" :mins 3.7 :head "async function loadRowActionsContent(host, fnId, context, opts) {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The migration in the concrete: the `⋯` row menu is a server partial in four contexts, and the client only builds the query string, caches the returned HTML per URL, and recomputes the one state that MUST be fresh (add-MI compatibility, against current `lookups`) — see [docs/EDITOR_ROW_ACTIONS.md](../EDITOR_ROW_ACTIONS.md) for why this popover moved and the others stayed JS.")
    (:key "editor/enterArgValueEditMode" :n 10 :gi 179 :defn "enterArgValueEditMode" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-edit-modes.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-edit-modes.js" :line 180 :lang "js" :mins 4.6 :head "function enterArgValueEditMode(arg, anchorEl) {" :see (("crud/render-repr" . "render-repr")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "How an edit actually happens. The control is not chosen here: the client POSTs the slot to `/api/value-form` and the graph's value-form registry answers with hiccup naming a widget — the editor routes on `data-form-widget` and knows no type tags at all. Note `onSaved`: a value change alters binding rows, so `lookups` is stale and the handler re-fetches rather than re-rendering.")
    (:key "editor/gdEnterDiffMode" :n 11 :gi 180 :defn "gdEnterDiffMode" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-diff-mode.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-diff-mode.js" :line 1283 :lang "js" :mins 3.6 :head "async function gdEnterDiffMode(otherBranch) {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "COMPARE MODE — THE diff surface, entered by picking the second branch (the Δ on a popover row; click again to exit). One fetch of the grouped `diff-view` payload, then pure annotation: Explorer rows badge +/±/−, namespace headers aggregate counts, a LENS BAR under the kind chips filters the tree by change type (`gdDiffSetLens` — only-changed, kind toggles, substantive-only, effects-touched-only), changed fn cards and args ring on the canvas (`gdDiffModeCardInfo` / `gdDiffSlotsForFn`), a selected changed fn gets an inspector DIFF PANEL (`gdDiffRenderInspectorSection` — old → new entries, effects delta, anchored 💬 threads), and the Δ chip persists the mode across reloads. The chip's menu is the review cockpit: Review & comments dialog, propose-current-for-review, merge-compared-in. Fns existing only on the compared branch inject as ghost rows; effect-set deltas come from a FULL per-branch registry comparison (`gdDiffEffectSetDelta` — the registry is branch-scoped), with the changed-refs signal (`gdDiffEffectsTouched`) as the anonymous-fn fallback. Note what it does NOT do: no data re-routing — every read and write stays on the current branch, so the user keeps editing with the lens on.")
    (:key "editor/gdApplyThemePayload" :n 12 :gi 181 :defn "gdApplyThemePayload" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-prefs.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-prefs.js" :line 361 :lang "js" :mins 1.9 :head "function gdApplyThemePayload(payload) {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A THEME (Settings → Appearance, docs/MARKETPLACE.md § 5) is a payload `{mode tokens fonts scale}` applied as INLINE custom properties on `<body>` — inline beats `body.theme-dark {…}`, so it wins in either base and clears cleanly. Read `sanitizeThemePayload` just above: a shared theme is another user's data, so only allow-listed tokens with colour literals get through; a `url()` or a second declaration is dropped. The mirror in localStorage is applied before first paint; the server preference reconciles after boot.")
    (:key "editor/gdApplyKeymap" :n 13 :gi 182 :defn "gdApplyKeymap" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-shortcuts.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-shortcuts.js" :line 81 :lang "js" :mins 1.3 :head "function gdApplyKeymap(bindings) {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A keyboard LAYOUT is an overrides map `{id {keys leader}}` over the shortcut registry. `_defaults` remembers each id's declared keys the first time it registers (reset is possible), and `registerShortcut` consults `_overrides` on every registration — so the canvas's late `g g` still lands on the user's keys. Settings → Keyboard renders `gdShortcutEntries()`; the which-key menu and the `?` cheatsheet show the rebound keys because they already read the registry.")
    (:key "editor/authFetch" :n 14 :gi 183 :defn "authFetch" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-auth.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-auth.js" :line 144 :lang "js" :mins 1.4 :head "async function authFetch(url, opts = {}) {" :see nil :refs (("editor/initGraph" . "initGraph")) :siblings nil
     :say "Every mutating call in the editor goes through this. It is 12 lines and worth all of them: a 401 clears the stored credential and re-opens the sign-in popover in place. The trap to internalize before you write editor code — `authFetch` and `authMutate` **resolve** on 4xx, they do not throw. A caller that only wraps them in `try/catch` counts a rejected write as a success.")
    (:key "editor/_tourTick" :n 15 :gi 184 :defn "_tourTick" :ns "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-tour.js" :file "resources/packages/app/editor/editor-tour.js" :line 677 :lang "js" :mins 3.9 :head "function _tourTick() {" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Closing the loop back to the user tutorial: the in-editor guided lessons are graph content (`app.tour/_tour-lessons`, served at `GET /api/tour`) and this file is only the engine that interprets them. Each tick clears the reader-state traps that make a lesson unfinishable — a collapsed Explorer, a kind lens hiding the very fn the step names, a target scrolled out of view — then polls the step's declarative check and advances. A tour that can dead-end is worse than no tour, which is what every guard here is paying for.")))
  (:id "services" :title "Services & the fleet" :status "toured" :mins 51.6 :summary "Desired-state service registry, the reconciler/supervisor, and the
             dynamic multi-executor fleet controller."
   :steps
   ((:key "services/extend-builder" :n 1 :gi 185 :defn "extend-builder" :ns "graphden.schema.services.schema" :file "src/graphden/schema/services/schema.clj" :line 299 :lang "clj" :mins 5.8 :head "(defn extend-builder" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/effective-pool-size" . "effective-pool-size"))
     :say "Start with what a service ROW is: this registers the `:service` entity plus the `:restart-policy` / `:cardinality` enums. A service is a desired-state `keep this fn-id running` declaration — not versioned, no args table — the thing everything downstream reconciles toward.")
    (:key "services/effective-pool-size" :n 2 :gi 186 :defn "effective-pool-size" :ns "graphden.schema.services.schema" :file "src/graphden/schema/services/schema.clj" :line 154 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn effective-pool-size" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/extend-builder" . "extend-builder"))
     :say "Resolves a row's cardinality into an advisory-lock slot count: `:per-pod` → no lock, `:singleton` → 1, `:pool` → N. You need this singleton-vs-per-pod-vs-pool model before the reconciler's lock gating makes sense.")
    (:key "services/diff-desired" :n 3 :gi 187 :defn "diff-desired" :ns "graphden.services.reconciler" :file "src/graphden/services/reconciler.clj" :line 77 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn diff-desired" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/reconcile-once!" . "reconcile-once!") ("services/start-service!" . "start-service!") ("services/endpoint-of" . "endpoint-of") ("services/check-liveness!" . "check-liveness!"))
     :say "The pure policy core: given the enabled DB rows (desired) and the in-process `running` set (actual), return `{:to-start :to-stop}`. It isolates the reconcile DECISION from side effects — the cleanest first look at the loop.")
    (:key "services/reconcile-once!" :n 4 :gi 188 :defn "reconcile-once!" :ns "graphden.services.reconciler" :file "src/graphden/services/reconciler.clj" :line 675 :lang "clj" :mins 3.7 :head "(defn reconcile-once!" :see nil :refs (("storage/create-listener" . "create-listener") ("boot/-main" . "-main")) :siblings (("services/diff-desired" . "diff-desired") ("services/start-service!" . "start-service!") ("services/endpoint-of" . "endpoint-of") ("services/check-liveness!" . "check-liveness!"))
     :say "One reconcile pass: read enabled rows, apply `diff-desired`, detect config drift, and race advisory-lock slots. This is BOTH the edge-triggered handler (fired by `:service` CRUD / NOTIFY) and the level-triggered periodic tick — the placeholder it recomputes each pass is exactly what heals a crashed lock-owner on the next tick.")
    (:key "services/start-service!" :n 5 :gi 189 :defn "start-service!" :ns "graphden.services.reconciler" :file "src/graphden/services/reconciler.clj" :line 161 :lang "clj" :mins 4.3 :head "(defn start-service!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/diff-desired" . "diff-desired") ("services/reconcile-once!" . "reconcile-once!") ("services/endpoint-of" . "endpoint-of") ("services/check-liveness!" . "check-liveness!"))
     :say "The supervisor that actually keeps a fn running: invoke the target fn via the executor and, on a startup failure, sleep + retry up to N times per the row's `:restart-policy`, stamping `:start-failed-at` when exhausted so reconcile will not busy-loop. The `how a service stays up` answer that closes out the services half.")
    (:key "services/endpoint-of" :n 6 :gi 190 :defn "endpoint-of" :ns "graphden.services.reconciler" :file "src/graphden/services/reconciler.clj" :line 261 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defn endpoint-of" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/diff-desired" . "diff-desired") ("services/reconcile-once!" . "reconcile-once!") ("services/start-service!" . "start-service!") ("services/check-liveness!" . "check-liveness!"))
     :say "A service that listens somewhere ANSWERS somewhere: the handle `:http-server` returns carries `{:endpoint {:port p}}` as metadata, and the pod that started it completes that with its own dialable host (the fleet `:executor-id`, or loopback) before writing this copy's `:service-instance` row — host, port, and a heartbeat it refreshes every tick. Read this next because it is the write side of service-to-service — the fact every consumer resolves.")
    (:key "services/dependency-closure" :n 7 :gi 191 :defn "dependency-closure" :ns "graphden.storage.protocol.generic-constraints" :file "src/graphden/storage/protocol/generic_constraints.clj" :line 125 :lang "clj" :mins 3.4 :head "(defn dependency-closure" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The write-time cycle check's closure, since a consumer named the editor's own listener: the ref's execution graph is loaded through `sp/resolve-execution-graph` (one recursive CTE) and walked in memory — the edges `forward-deps-of` follows, identity edges skipped, constraint-vector type names resolved per round. No per-fn queries, no visit cap; the old walk took seconds and then refused as `chain-too-deep`.")
    (:key "services/check-liveness!" :n 8 :gi 192 :defn "check-liveness!" :ns "graphden.services.reconciler" :file "src/graphden/services/reconciler.clj" :line 457 :lang "clj" :mins 2.5 :head "(defn- check-liveness!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/diff-desired" . "diff-desired") ("services/reconcile-once!" . "reconcile-once!") ("services/start-service!" . "start-service!") ("services/endpoint-of" . "endpoint-of"))
     :say "The per-tick liveness pass: a handle may also carry `:alive?` and `:exit` (http-kit's listener status; a `:future`'s thread state and how it ended), so a copy that died IN PLACE is caught here — its row deleted, its lock released — and the row's `:restart-policy` finally means what it says: `:always` restarts any exit, `:on-failure` only a throw, `:never` parks it. Live copies just get their heartbeat.")
    (:key "services/resolve-endpoint" :n 9 :gi 193 :defn "resolve-endpoint" :ns "graphden.services.endpoint" :file "src/graphden/services/endpoint.clj" :line 86 :lang "clj" :mins 2.0 :head "(defn resolve-endpoint" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The read side: a consumer names a service through a `:fn-ref` slot (`:service-endpoint :service :orders-service` — the fn's identity, never evaluated, not a dependency, so two services may name each other) and this turns the id into `{:host :port :url}` — a LIVE instance (fresh heartbeat, this pod's copy first) of the fn's row on the caller's branch, else the addon-installed `resolver` (a cloud app-route's public origin), else an honest `:service/not-running`. The web/service templates (`:service-get`) compose it with http-client; nothing here dials.")
    (:key "services/run-traced!" :n 10 :gi 194 :defn "run-traced!" :ns "graphden.crud.fn-execution.trace" :file "src/graphden/crud/fn_execution/trace.clj" :line 103 :lang "clj" :mins 1.7 :head "(defn run-traced!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "A call across the wire stays one tree: a persisted run's `:service-get` sends `X-Graphden-Trace` (trace id + its own execution id), and the listener runs the request through this — a fresh execution id bound for the handler's own outbound calls, a path trace, and the outcome persisted as an execution of the handler fn linked to the caller. No header, no row: ordinary traffic pays nothing. `get-execution` then lists a run's `:children`.")
    (:key "services/extend-builder~2" :n 11 :gi 195 :defn "extend-builder" :ns "graphden.schema.queue.schema" :file "src/graphden/schema/queue/schema.clj" :line 74 :lang "clj" :mins 3.6 :head "(defn extend-builder" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The other way two services talk: a queue. One Postgres table with the standard job-queue shape — `pending`/`dead`, attempts, a due time, a visibility lock — that `storage/queue`'s four primitives drive (`FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` for the claim, NOTIFY to wake a waiting taker). The consumer LOOP is graph composition in that module's fns.edn, with the backend as fn-typed slots — read this to see why a broker was not needed.")
    (:key "services/placement-for" :n 12 :gi 196 :defn "placement-for" :ns "graphden.fleet.placement" :file "src/graphden/fleet/placement.clj" :line 11 :lang "clj" :mins 1.1 :head "(defn placement-for" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Opens the fleet half with its shared source of truth: the `:placement` row mapping `(org, entry-fn-id)` → the executor holding that cell, highest-epoch wins. Every fleet component reads or writes this map, so it is the right foundation before routing and packing.")
    (:key "services/forward-or-nil" :n 13 :gi 197 :defn "forward-or-nil" :ns "graphden.fleet.router" :file "src/graphden/fleet/router.clj" :line 57 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defn forward-or-nil" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The internal forward-hop: when a request lands on a pod that does not hold the target cell, look up the holder in the placement map and HTTP-forward there instead of returning `421`. How the placement map becomes live cross-fleet routing.")
    (:key "services/cell-weight" :n 14 :gi 198 :defn "cell-weight" :ns "graphden.fleet.metrics" :file "src/graphden/fleet/metrics.clj" :line 57 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defn cell-weight" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Folds a cell's structural cost (forward-closure fn-count) and live load (the org's in-flight executions) into the single scalar the packer and rebalancer order by. The load signal that drives every placement decision below.")
    (:key "services/pack" :n 15 :gi 199 :defn "pack" :ns "graphden.fleet.packer" :file "src/graphden/fleet/packer.clj" :line 69 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn pack" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The LPT (longest-processing-time-first) greedy packer: sort cells heaviest-first and drop each on the least-loaded executor — the classic makespan 2-approximation. It answers `what SHOULD the placement be` for a from-scratch layout.")
    (:key "services/rebalance" :n 16 :gi 200 :defn "rebalance" :ns "graphden.fleet.rebalance" :file "src/graphden/fleet/rebalance.clj" :line 81 :lang "clj" :mins 5.0 :head "(defn rebalance" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The churn-minimising planner: rather than diff against a fresh pack, do steepest-descent (move one cell busiest → lightest while it strictly reduces spread) with a `:min-improvement` hysteresis floor, emitting the FEWEST moves. How a running fleet is nudged toward balance without thrashing.")
    (:key "services/plan-tick" :n 17 :gi 201 :defn "plan-tick" :ns "graphden.fleet.control-loop" :file "src/graphden/fleet/control_loop.clj" :line 63 :lang "clj" :mins 2.7 :head "(defn plan-tick" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/run-tick!" . "run-tick!"))
     :say "The pure brain of one control pass: always emit initial placements for unheld cells, but gate rebalance moves behind `:sustain-ticks` so imbalance must PERSIST (not just spike) before cells move. Composes the packer + rebalancer into the actual control policy.")
    (:key "services/run-tick!" :n 18 :gi 202 :defn "run-tick!" :ns "graphden.fleet.control-loop" :file "src/graphden/fleet/control_loop.clj" :line 177 :lang "clj" :mins 2.4 :head "(defn run-tick!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("services/plan-tick" . "plan-tick"))
     :say "The side-effecting driver the leader-locked component runs each tick: read live cells + current placement, call `plan-tick`, then realise each initial placement and sustained move through the `move-fn` seam. The loop that ties the whole fleet together.")
    (:key "services/move-cell!" :n 19 :gi 203 :defn "move-cell!" :ns "graphden.fleet.controller" :file "src/graphden/fleet/controller.clj" :line 67 :lang "clj" :mins 3.7 :head "(defn move-cell!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Executes a single relocation as `run-tick!`'s `move-fn`: the load → flip-epoch → evict dance that keeps the cell continuously served (target compiles first, placement epoch bumps to reroute, source evicts last). How an abstract `move` decision safely becomes a live cell migration across pods.")))
  (:id "platform" :title "Platform seams & hardening" :status "toured" :mins 27.5 :summary "The seams the multi-tenant addon plugs into (docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md)
             and the hardening layers the core itself carries — effect gate,
             SSRF egress guard, alerting, BYO executor."
   :steps
   ((:key "platform/platform-tier?" :n 1 :gi 204 :defn "platform-tier?" :ns "graphden.tenancy.context" :file "src/graphden/tenancy/context.clj" :line 43 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defn platform-tier?" :see nil :refs (("boot/read-config" . "read-config") ("constellation/install-org-cap-fn!" . "install-org-cap-fn!")) :siblings (("platform/notify!" . "notify!") ("constellation/install-org-cap-fn!" . "install-org-cap-fn!") ("constellation/tenancy-addon-active?" . "tenancy-addon-active?"))
     :say "The one tenancy namespace that lives in core: `*current-org*` is the per-request thread-local the private addon binds, and this predicate is how core code asks `am I the platform, or a tenant?` without depending on the policy. Everything org-scoped downstream keys off this seam.")
    (:key "platform/notify!" :n 2 :gi 205 :defn "notify!" :ns "graphden.tenancy.context" :file "src/graphden/tenancy/context.clj" :line 176 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn notify!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("platform/platform-tier?" . "platform-tier?") ("constellation/install-org-cap-fn!" . "install-org-cap-fn!") ("constellation/tenancy-addon-active?" . "tenancy-addon-active?"))
     :say "The notification seam — the same install-a-hook shape as the capability predicates, pointed the other way: core RAISES a domain event (`:package-moderated` with the decided row) and the addon decides who is told and how (the publishing org's owner, through the accounts Mailer, on the trusted origin). Without an addon the event is dropped, and the sink's result is never inspected — a mail failure is the sender's to log, not the domain write's to undo.")
    (:key "platform/single-token-provider" :n 3 :gi 206 :defn "single-token-provider" :ns "graphden.auth.provider" :file "src/graphden/auth/provider.clj" :line 63 :lang "clj" :mins 1.0 :head "(defn single-token-provider" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Auth is provider-driven: core ships this single-token default behind the `:auth/provider` Integrant key, and the addon swaps in its session/user provider. Note the constant-time compare — the default is small, but it is still a real credential check.")
    (:key "platform/cloud-forbidden-effects" :n 4 :gi 207 :defn "cloud-forbidden-effects" :ns "graphden.executor.compile-runtime" :file "src/graphden/executor/compile_runtime.clj" :line 1217 :lang "clj" :mins 3.0 :head "(def cloud-forbidden-effects" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("executor/execute~2" . "execute") ("executor/registry" . "registry") ("executor/rebuild!" . "rebuild!"))
     :say "The effect gate's registry of what a sandboxed graph must NOT do — env / io / network / process / raw-sql — with the docstring explaining why `:raw-sql` exists separately from `:db`. The gate mechanism (`record-effect!` throwing outside `*allowed-effects*`) lives in core; the addon only chooses the policy sets.")
    (:key "platform/check-target!" :n 5 :gi 208 :defn "check-target!" :ns "graphden.clients.egress" :file "src/graphden/clients/egress.clj" :line 86 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defn check-target!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The SSRF egress guard a tenant's outbound call passes before dialing: fail-closed resolution of the hostname, rejecting loopback / RFC1918 / link-local / cloud-metadata targets. Pair it with `validating-dns` below in the same file — the connect-time re-check that closes the DNS-rebind TOCTOU.")
    (:key "platform/sanitize-hiccup" :n 6 :gi 209 :defn "sanitize-hiccup" :ns "graphden.web.hiccup-sanitize" :file "src/graphden/web/hiccup_sanitize.clj" :line 116 :lang "clj" :mins 2.9 :head "(defn sanitize-hiccup" :see (("crud/render-repr" . "render-repr")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "An allowlist walk over an EDN hiccup tree — permitted tags, permitted attrs, a depth cap and a node budget, returning nil for 'no representable output'. This is the gate on every path where GRAPH-authored markup reaches the editor's own DOM: typed value representations, component previews, server partials rendering user content. Anything not on the list collapses to escaped text rather than being rejected loudly — the sanitizer's job is to make the surface safe, not to argue with the author.")
    (:key "platform/count!" :n 7 :gi 210 :defn "count!" :ns "graphden.util.counters" :file "src/graphden/util/counters.clj" :line 65 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn count!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "Observability starts smaller than you expect: a process-global, monotonic `{event → long}` atom. Everything in docs/MONITORING.md — the usage rollups, the Prometheus scrape, the alerter's window below — is a diff of two `snapshot`s of this map. Monotonic on purpose: readers diff rather than reset, so two of them can never invalidate each other's window.")
    (:key "platform/decide" :n 8 :gi 211 :defn "decide" :ns "graphden.monitoring.alerts" :file "src/graphden/monitoring/alerts.clj" :line 59 :lang "clj" :mins 3.3 :head "(defn decide" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The built-in alerter's pure policy: given the last window's counters and the previous state, decide fire / resolve / stay quiet. All the side effects (scheduler, webhook POST) live elsewhere — this is the testable core of docs/MONITORING.md's second alerting path.")
    (:key "platform/start-relay!" :n 9 :gi 212 :defn "start-relay!" :ns "graphden.system.sse" :file "src/graphden/system/sse.clj" :line 168 :lang "clj" :mins 2.0 :head "(defn start-relay!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The SSE invalidation relay: the hosted side fans branch/org invalidation events out to subscribed external executors so a BYO executor's compiled registry stays fresh without polling. The freshness half of the BYO story.")
    (:key "platform/RemoteStorage" :n 10 :gi 213 :defn "RemoteStorage" :ns "graphden.storage.remote.core" :file "src/graphden/storage/remote/core.clj" :line 112 :lang "clj" :mins 3.8 :head "(defrecord RemoteStorage" :see (("storage/StorageCRUD" . "StorageCRUD")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The read side of BYO: a `StorageCRUD` leaf whose rows are an ATOM of `{entity → [row …]}` fetched from the hub over HTTP, with every write arm throwing `read-only!`. The customer's executor compiles and runs the same graph while the hub stays the only writer — and `refresh!` swaps in a fresh bundle without rebuilding the record, which is what the SSE relay below triggers.")
    (:key "platform/start-byo!" :n 11 :gi 214 :defn "start-byo!" :ns "graphden.byo" :file "src/graphden/byo.clj" :line 130 :lang "clj" :mins 6.3 :head "(defn start-byo!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The customer-hosted executor assembly: RemoteStorage over HTTP as the read-only graph source, the SSE source for invalidation, and a direct http-server — the same core, assembled for someone else's hardware. Closes the platform block: the seams above are exactly what make this assembly possible.")))
  (:id "accounts" :title "Accounts — the open identity module" :status "toured" :mins 15.4 :summary "The opt-in identity layer (docs/ACCOUNTS.md): one account, many
             sign-in identities (password / Google / GitHub / Telegram),
             hashed sessions, email verification, TOTP 2FA, and the /auth/*
             + /login surface — all OFF unless the addon fragment is enabled."
   :steps
   ((:key "accounts/resolve-social-identity!" :n 1 :gi 215 :defn "resolve-social-identity!" :ns "graphden.accounts.core" :file "src/graphden/accounts/core.clj" :line 169 :lang "clj" :mins 2.6 :head "(defn resolve-social-identity!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("accounts/mint-session!" . "mint-session!"))
     :say "The heart of account-LINKING: a social sign-in resolves to an existing identity, auto-links onto an account that owns the same VERIFIED email, or creates a fresh account — in that order. One person, many sign-in methods, one stable account-id for authz.")
    (:key "accounts/mint-session!" :n 2 :gi 216 :defn "mint-session!" :ns "graphden.accounts.core" :file "src/graphden/accounts/core.clj" :line 212 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(defn mint-session!" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("accounts/resolve-social-identity!" . "resolve-social-identity!"))
     :say "Sessions are rows, not JWTs: the raw token exists exactly once (the return value); storage keeps only its SHA-256. `kind` overloads the same row for verify links and pending-2FA steps — kinds outside nil/\"api\" never authenticate, so a verify link can't become a bearer.")
    (:key "accounts/accounts-provider" :n 3 :gi 217 :defn "accounts-provider" :ns "graphden.accounts.provider" :file "src/graphden/accounts/provider.clj" :line 64 :lang "clj" :mins 0.9 :head "(defn accounts-provider" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The module's AuthProvider: bearer OR the HttpOnly gd_session cookie (an OAuth redirect can't carry a bearer) → an org-AGNOSTIC principal. Org is deliberately absent — the tenancy layer resolves it from membership, which is what keeps this module open-core.")
    (:key "accounts/make-router" :n 4 :gi 218 :defn "make-router" :ns "graphden.accounts.routes" :file "src/graphden/accounts/routes.clj" :line 509 :lang "clj" :mins 8.9 :head "(defn make-router" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The whole HTTP surface as one route-collection Ring router: password + social + telegram callbacks, verify links, identities list/unlink, TOTP enroll/confirm/login-step, and the /login + /account pages. Installed before the graph router; returns nil to fall through, so an instance without the addon never sees it. The page/email PRESENTATION renders through the injected graph seam (`app.auth-pages` on the platform ctx) with the built-in Clojure shell as the graph-outage fallback — see `graph-page` just above.")
    (:key "accounts/valid?" :n 5 :gi 219 :defn "valid?" :ns "graphden.accounts.totp" :file "src/graphden/accounts/totp.clj" :line 102 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn valid?" :see nil :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "RFC 6238 in ~100 lines: base32, HOTP, a ±1-step skew window and a constant-time compare, verified against the RFC's reference vectors. The enrollment/confirm flow that uses it lives in accounts.core.")))
  (:id "constellation" :title "Constellation — the repos around the monorepo" :status "toured" :mins 12.5 :summary "graphden is one of seven repositories, and the seams the other
   six plug into all live here: addon config fragments, the capability
   predicates, the external-package manifest, and a CI guard for the vars a
   private repo reaches. This block tours those seams and tells the other
   side of each — what graphden-cloud, graphden-tenancy, graphden-mathx,
   graphden-examples, graphden-internal and the org `.github` repo contribute,
   how they pin each other, and how a release moves through all of them."
   :steps
   ((:key "constellation/addon-config-resources" :n 1 :gi 220 :defn "addon-config-resources" :ns "graphden.system.config" :file "src/graphden/system/config.clj" :line 57 :lang "clj" :mins 1.4 :head "(defn addon-config-resources" :see (("boot/read-config" . "read-config")) :refs nil :siblings (("boot/read-config" . "read-config") ("constellation/merge-addon" . "merge-addon"))
     :say "The whole open-core split hangs off ONE environment variable. Core runs as-is with it unset (single-tenant, every seam at its default). Production sets `GRAPHDEN_ADDON_CONFIGS=graphden/tenancy/addon.edn,cloud/addon.edn,cloud/prod.edn` — three classpath resources from two OTHER repos, applied in order. The map of the constellation, and the direction of every arrow: **graphden-cloud** depends on **graphden-tenancy** and **graphden**; tenancy depends on graphden; graphden depends only on **graphden-mathx**. Nothing public ever depends on anything private, and nothing depends on graphden-cloud.")
    (:key "constellation/merge-addon" :n 2 :gi 221 :defn "merge-addon" :ns "graphden.system.config" :file "src/graphden/system/config.clj" :line 68 :lang "clj" :mins 1.9 :head "(defn- merge-addon" :see (("boot/context" . "context") ("boot/dispatch-first" . "dispatch-first")) :refs nil :siblings (("boot/read-config" . "read-config") ("constellation/addon-config-resources" . "addon-config-resources"))
     :say "How a fragment attaches: its `:graphden/require` directive loads the addon's namespaces (so their `ig/init-key` defmethods exist — tenancy registers about twenty-five: `:org/scoped-storage`, `:tenancy/request-scope`, `:tenancy/execute-guard`, `:auth/multi-tenant-provider`, `:tenancy/app-router`, …), then the rest deep-merges over the core config. graphden-tenancy is a pure source library (33 namespaces under `graphden.tenancy.*`, hub `addon.clj`, plus the `tenancy-admin` package — deliberately NOT depending on `app`); graphden-cloud adds only three more namespaces (usage metering, a quota execute-guard, a db helper) and the `landing` package that IS graphden.dev's own site. The rule in docs/PACKAGE_DISTRIBUTION.md § 16: cloud vs self-hosted is a MODE of one binary, never a fork.")
    (:key "constellation/install-org-cap-fn!" :n 3 :gi 222 :defn "install-org-cap-fn!" :ns "graphden.tenancy.context" :file "src/graphden/tenancy/context.clj" :line 130 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defn install-org-cap-fn!" :see (("platform/platform-tier?" . "platform-tier?")) :refs nil :siblings (("platform/platform-tier?" . "platform-tier?") ("platform/notify!" . "notify!") ("constellation/tenancy-addon-active?" . "tenancy-addon-active?"))
     :say "The only policy seam core exposes as a mutable slot. `graphden.tenancy.context` is the ONE tenancy namespace kept in this repo: the `*current-org*` thread-local, the tier predicates, and two default-deny capability predicates the addon installs at boot (`addon.clj` calls this with `org-admin/current-has-org-capability?` and its platform twin with `grant/current-has-platform-capability?`). Every authz gate in core reads through them — so a self-hosted instance denies nothing and a cloud instance denies by grant, from the same call sites. docs/TENANCY_SEAM.md is the mechanism half; the policy half's ADRs live in the private repo's `docs/PLATFORM_NOTES.md`.")
    (:key "constellation/tenancy-addon-active?" :n 4 :gi 223 :defn "tenancy-addon-active?" :ns "graphden.tenancy.context" :file "src/graphden/tenancy/context.clj" :line 138 :lang "clj" :mins 1.2 :head "(defn tenancy-addon-active?" :see nil :refs nil :siblings (("platform/platform-tier?" . "platform-tier?") ("platform/notify!" . "notify!") ("constellation/install-org-cap-fn!" . "install-org-cap-fn!"))
     :say "…and the one fact server-rendered copy is allowed to branch on: is the addon installed at all. Not a config flag, not a tier — the installed predicate itself. The editor derives the same answer from capability headers, so nothing is duplicated client-side; a page that says 'organisation' only when there are organisations gets it from here.")
    (:key "constellation/every-addon-facing-var-still-resolves-test" :n 5 :gi 224 :defn "every-addon-facing-var-still-resolves-test" :ns "test/graphden/open_core_seam_test.clj" :file "test/graphden/open_core_seam_test.clj" :line 33 :lang "clj" :mins 1.8 :head "(deftest every-addon-facing-var-still-resolves-test" :see (("crud/create-entity" . "create-entity")) :refs nil :siblings nil
     :say "The guard that makes a private consumer safe to have. Most vars the tenancy addon reaches have NO caller in graphden — they exist for the addon — so a refactor here can drop one and this suite stays green; the break would surface at pin-bump time in a repo this CI cannot see (it did: splitting `crud/entities.clj` lost `view-impl-filter`). `tools/open-core-seam.edn` lists every such var, generated from the addon's own requires, and this test resolves each one. Rename or move one → red HERE, in the same landing.")
    (:key "constellation/read-manifest" :n 6 :gi 225 :defn "read-manifest" :ns "graphden.packages.manifest" :file "src/graphden/packages/manifest.clj" :line 32 :lang "clj" :mins 1.3 :head "(defn read-manifest" :see (("boot/packages" . "packages") ("packages/load-default-packages" . "load-default-packages")) :refs nil :siblings (("constellation/extra-deps" . "extra-deps"))
     :say "The other kind of sibling: an external Type-2 package (code + fn-defs in its own repo). `resources/executor-packages.edn` is the operator's single data file naming them — today exactly one, **graphden-mathx** (`:gcd` + `:gcd-with-12`, the smallest package that proves the path end-to-end). `build.clj` bundles the listed libs into the uberjar; `:app/packages` loads them by name at boot. A name-only entry means the package is already on the classpath by other means.")
    (:key "constellation/extra-deps" :n 7 :gi 226 :defn "extra-deps" :ns "graphden.packages.manifest" :file "src/graphden/packages/manifest.clj" :line 48 :lang "clj" :mins 1.5 :head "(defn extra-deps" :see (("packages/install!" . "install!")) :refs nil :siblings (("constellation/read-manifest" . "read-manifest"))
     :say "Where the manifest meets tools.deps. The `{lib coord}` map the build basis adds — and the reason `bb pkg-pin-sync` exists: `deps.edn` and the manifest must name the same `:git/sha`, or the lint/test classpath and the shipped jar disagree. Its sibling `bb pkg-copy-sync` guards CONTENT: `external-packages/{mathx,examples}` are plain in-tree copies (no submodules — § 15 AD-3) that `:override-deps` / the test paths actually exercise, checked byte-identical against the standalone checkouts when they are present. **graphden-examples** rides only that way — 28 lesson modules on the dev/test path, never in a production graph.")
    (:key "constellation/-main" :n 8 :gi 227 :defn "-main" :ns "graphden.executor-runtime.core" :file "src/graphden/executor_runtime/core.clj" :line 187 :lang "clj" :mins 2.1 :head "(defn -main" :see (("boot/boot!" . "boot!") ("boot/read-config" . "read-config")) :refs nil :siblings (("boot/-main" . "-main") ("boot/boot!" . "boot!"))
     :say "Same `-main`, second reading: graphden-cloud's `:run` alias starts THIS namespace — it ships no main of its own — and its Dockerfile pins the same JRE digest as ours. The release train (`bb release` in graphden-cloud, every step idempotent): bump tenancy's graphden pin → push tenancy `main` → wait for its CI on the self-hosted runner → bump both pins in cloud → regenerate the baked tutorial → assert the **pin diamond** is closed (cloud and tenancy must name ONE graphden sha, or prod runs tenancy against a graphden it was never tested on; checked again in `deploy.yml`) → push, and the push to cloud `main` IS the prod deploy → fast-forward this repo's public `main` and tag `prod-<stamp>`. The two repos without code edges: **graphden-internal** (private planning, legal, the RU translations of the tutorial and of THIS tour — its bake re-invokes our `bb devtour` — and the CI-runner setup) and **graphden-dotgithub** (org-level issue / PR templates, no reusable workflows). `graphden-wt/` beside them is not a repo but the worktree root the merge queue parks agents in (dev/wtq/).")))))
